var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#Causal-highlighting","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"DAGMakie highlights paths and adjustment sets that you already computed. Ancestor / adjustment colouring follows dagitty-style roles (Textor et al., 2016, IJE). Identification algorithms live in CausalInference.jl (or CausalDynamics.jl façades). Pass the resulting sets into the plot helpers — the core package does not pull CausalInference; load it when you want registry identification helpers alongside GraphMakie ≥0.6 (supported since CausalInference 0.19.4).\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n# From CausalInference.find_min_backdoor_adjustment(g, 2, 3) — or hard-code Z\nadjustment = Set([1])\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_backdoor(g, 2, 3; adjustment = adjustment, labels = labels)\nfig\nFigure()\n\ndagplot_adjustment accepts an explicit set the same way:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_adjustment(g, 2, 3; adjustment = Set([1]), labels = labels)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Causal highlighting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#Smart-/-dagitty-style-colours","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"Pass color_by=:ancestors with exposure and outcome to colour nodes like dagitty “highlight ancestors”. Variables outside the ancestral closure of exposure ∪ outcome are grayed out; shared ancestors (often on backdoors) are marked in red.\n\nColour Role\nSeagreen Exposure\nRoyal blue Outcome\nMedium seagreen Ancestor of exposure only\nSteel blue Ancestor of outcome only\nIndian red Ancestor of both\nLight gray Outside the ancestral closure\n\nAncestor sets use CausalInference.jl when it is loaded; otherwise Graphs reverse-BFS. color_by=:adjustment also thickens a backdoor adjustment set (needs CausalInference, or pass adjustment=).","title":"Smart / dagitty-style colours"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#Default-vs-color_by-(confounding-triangle)","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"Same graph, plain steel-blue nodes versus dagitty-style roles. Here Z is an ancestor of both exposure X and outcome Y (indian red).\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    fig = Figure(size = (900, 360))\n    ax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Default\")\n    ax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"color_by = :ancestors\")\n    dagplot!(ax1, g; labels = labels)\n    dagplot!(ax2, g; color_by = :ancestors, exposure = 2, outcome = 3, labels = labels)\n    fig\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Default vs color_by (confounding triangle)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#Full-role-palette","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"A slightly richer DAG shows every ancestor role at once: A affects only the exposure, B only the outcome, Z both, and W is irrelevant.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# A → X ← Z → Y ← B, X → Y; W isolated\ng = SimpleDiGraph(6)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 4)  # A → X\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 4)  # Z → X\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 5)  # Z → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 3, 5)  # B → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 4, 5)  # X → Y\nlabels = [\"A\", \"Z\", \"B\", \"X\", \"Y\", \"W\"]\n\nroles = classify_smart_roles(g, 4, 5)\n@show roles\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    # Place X below the Z→Y chord so A→X and Z→X do not cross it.\n    layout = Point2f[\n        (0.0, -2.7),  # A\n        (0.0, -0.9),  # Z\n        (0.0, 0.9),   # B\n        (2.6, -2.2),  # X (below Z→Y so parent edges do not cross it)\n        (5.2, 0.0),   # Y\n        (0.0, 2.7),   # W\n    ]\n    fig, ax, p = dagplot_smart(g, 4, 5;\n        labels = labels,\n        layout = layout,\n        figure_size = (700, 420),\n    )\n    ax.title = \"A: anc. exposure · B: anc. outcome · Z: both · W: irrelevant\"\n    fig\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Full role palette"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#Emphasise-an-adjustment-set","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"color_by=:adjustment keeps the ancestor colours and draws a thicker dark-red outline on adjustment nodes. Pass adjustment= explicitly when CausalInference is not loaded (as in this documentation build).\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    fig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n        color_by = :adjustment,\n        exposure = 2,\n        outcome = 3,\n        adjustment = Set([1]),  # Z\n        labels = labels,\n    )\n    ax.title = \"color_by = :adjustment (Z emphasised)\"\n    fig\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWith CausalInference available in your environment you can omit adjustment and let the extension compute a minimal backdoor set:\n\nusing CausalInference  # activates DAGMakieCausalInferenceExt\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g; color_by = :adjustment, exposure = 2, outcome = 3, labels = labels)\n\nThat call draws the same emphasis as below (for this DAG the extension returns Z). Because docs/Project.toml omits optional weak dependencies such as CausalInference (which supports GraphMakie 0.6 from version 0.19.4 onwards), this example passes the equivalent explicit adjustment set:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    fig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n        color_by = :adjustment,\n        exposure = 2,\n        outcome = 3,\n        adjustment = Set([1]),  # same as find_min_backdoor_adjustment(g, 2, 3)\n        labels = labels,\n    )\n    ax.title = \"color_by = :adjustment (Z from backdoor set)\"\n    fig\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Emphasise an adjustment set"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/causal/#d-Separation-titles","page":"Causal highlighting","text":"Pass separated from dsep / d_separated for axis titles. Use a fork X  Z  Y (no direct X  Y): conditioning on Z d-separates X and Y. A confounding triangle with X  Y keeps them d-connected.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = fork_graph([\"X\", \"Z\", \"Y\"])  # X ← Z → Y\n# X ⊥ Y | Z (Z blocks the only path)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_dsep(g, 1, 3, Set([2]); separated = true, labels = labels)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nCombine color_by colours with a d-separation title when teaching forks:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = fork_graph([\"X\", \"Z\", \"Y\"])\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    fig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n        color_by = :ancestors,\n        exposure = 1,\n        outcome = 3,\n        labels = labels,\n    )\n    ax.title = \"X ⊥ Y | Z (d-separated) · color_by ancestors\"\n    fig\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"d-Separation titles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#Temporal-and-skeletons","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"","title":"Temporal & skeletons"},{"category":"section","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#Time-indexed-and-mixed-temporal-plots","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"","title":"Time-indexed and mixed temporal plots"},{"category":"section","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#Temporal-smart-role-propagation","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"","title":"Temporal smart-role propagation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#Undirected-skeletons","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"","title":"Undirected skeletons"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.time_indexed_layout","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"time_indexed_layout(n_variables, n_times; dx=2.0, dy=1.5, origin=(0.0, 0.0))\n\nGrid layout for time-unrolled causal graphs.\n\nNode order must be outer loop over occasions t = 1:n_times and inner loop over variables v = 1:n_variables (the indexing used by CausalDynamics unroll_temporal_dag). Column t is at x = origin[1] + (t - 1) * dx; variable row v is at y = origin[2] - (v - 1) * dy.\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Point2f} of length n_variables * n_times\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.time_indexed_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.dagplot_time_indexed","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"dagplot_time_indexed(g, n_variables, n_times; kwargs...)\n\ndagplot with time_indexed_layout for graphs unrolled over time.\n\nPass nlabels with one label per node in the same (t, variable) order as time_indexed_layout. When color_by is set, exposure / outcome / ancestor roles propagate across each variable row (see propagate_temporal_smart_roles). exposure and outcome may be node indices or (variable_index, time_index) tuples. Remaining keywords go to dagplot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_time_indexed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.temporal_layout","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"temporal_layout(node_keys; onset_times, dx, dy, origin)\n\nCompute positions for a temporal graph. node_keys contains (variable, time) pairs. A pointwise key (v, t::Integer) is placed at column t; a single-node key (v, nothing) is placed at its entry in onset_times (default 0). Rows follow the order in which variable names first occur. Placement is a display fact only: it does not encode temporal support, endurance, or ontology.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.temporal_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.dagplot_temporal","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"dagplot_temporal(g, node_keys; onset_times,\n    temporal_supports, value_representations, graph_kind, kwargs...)\n\nPlot a temporal graph whose keys mix pointwise (v, t) nodes and single-node (v, nothing) keys placed at onset_times. Unlike dagplot_time_indexed, this API does not require a complete rectangular time grid. Markers follow value_representations (see marker_for_value_representation); temporal_supports is accepted for callers that carry it but does not change the glyph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_temporal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.interval_summary_node_marker","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"interval_summary_node_marker()\n\nReturn the Makie marker used when value_representation = :interval_summary under the package visual convention. Labels or support bars identify the interval summarised; shape alone does not establish support or ontology.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.interval_summary_node_marker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.marker_for_value_representation","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"marker_for_value_representation(repr)\n\nGlyph from declared value representation. Only :interval_summary selects the rounded rectangle under the package convention. Attributes, trajectories, states, and events use a circle. Temporal support and referent identity never select the shape.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.marker_for_value_representation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.resolve_temporal_exposure_outcome","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"resolve_temporal_exposure_outcome(exposure, outcome, n_variables) -> (Int, Int)\n\nAccept node indices (Int) or (variable, time) tuples for exposure / outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.resolve_temporal_exposure_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.propagate_temporal_smart_roles","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"propagate_temporal_smart_roles(\n    roles, n_variables, n_times; exposure, outcome\n) -> Vector{SmartNodeRole}\n\nPropagate per-node SmartNodeRoles across each variable row so Treatment[t] / Outcome[t] styling applies at every occasion on the estimand path (see issue #5).\n\nThe exposure variable row → all SmartExposure\nThe outcome variable row → all SmartOutcome\nOther rows → strongest ancestral role among occasions (both > X-only > Y-only > gray)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.propagate_temporal_smart_roles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.smart_style_for_temporal_graph","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"smart_style_for_temporal_graph(\n    g, n_variables, n_times, exposure, outcome; mode=:ancestors, adjustment=nothing\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nDagitty-style colours on a time-unrolled graph, with roles propagated across variable rows (see propagate_temporal_smart_roles).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.smart_style_for_temporal_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.temporal_role_styling","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"temporal_role_styling(\n    g, n_variables, n_times; exposure, outcome, color_by=:ancestors, adjustment=nothing\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nConvenience wrapper returning (node_color, nlabels_color, node_strokewidth, node_strokecolor, roles, adjustment) for manual dagplot / dagplot_time_indexed calls (#5).\n\nexposure and outcome may be node indices or (variable_index, time_index) pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.temporal_role_styling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.apply_temporal_smart_kwargs","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"apply_temporal_smart_kwargs(\n    g, n_variables, n_times; color_by, exposure, outcome, adjustment, kwargs...\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nLike apply_smart_kwargs, but propagates exposure / outcome / ancestor roles across variable rows on time-unrolled graphs (#5).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.apply_temporal_smart_kwargs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.temporal_node_index","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"temporal_node_index(n_variables, variable, time) -> Int\n\nLinear node index for (variable, time) in a time-unrolled graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.temporal_node_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.temporal_variable_time","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"temporal_variable_time(node, n_variables) -> (variable, time)\n\nInverse of temporal_node_index for 1-based node indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.temporal_variable_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.digraph_skeleton","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"digraph_skeleton(g::AbstractGraph) -> SimpleGraph\n\nUndirected skeleton of a directed graph: keep an undirected edge {i,j} whenever g has i → j, j → i, or both.\n\nUseful for CPDAG-style PC output that encodes an undirected edge as a pair of opposing directed edges. Prefer this over plotting both arrows. See dagplot_skeleton for a ready-made figure with undirected stroke styling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.digraph_skeleton"},{"category":"function","location":"api/temporal_and_skeletons/#DAGMakie.dagplot_skeleton","page":"Temporal & skeletons","text":"dagplot_skeleton(g; kwargs...)\n\nPlot the undirected skeleton of g (digraph_skeleton).\n\nArrowheads are suppressed and edges use UNDIRECTED_EDGE_COLOR unless overridden. Remaining keywords go to dagplot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_skeleton"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Layout-and-themes","page":"Layout & themes","text":"","title":"Layout & themes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Layout-utilities","page":"Layout & themes","text":"","title":"Layout utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Label-alignment-and-sizing","page":"Layout & themes","text":"","title":"Label alignment and sizing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Edge-routing","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Edges are straight by default. Per-edge bows use edge_routing on dagplot / dagplot! (see Basic Plotting).","title":"Edge routing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Themes-and-styling","page":"Layout & themes","text":"","title":"Themes and styling"},{"category":"section","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#Node-type-styling","page":"Layout & themes","text":"","title":"Node type styling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.compute_graph_layout","page":"Layout & themes","text":"compute_graph_layout(g; kwargs...)\n\nResolve deterministic positions and feedback-edge routing metadata for a graph.\n\nIf layout is provided, those positions are respected, but the graph is still classified so cyclic feedback routing can be applied consistently.\n\nWhen layout is omitted and the graph is a 3-node transitive triangle (unique source with edges to both other nodes plus an edge between them), an apex-top pedagogical layout is used so the shortcut edge remains visible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_graph_layout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DAGLayoutResult","page":"Layout & themes","text":"DAGLayoutResult\n\nResolved node positions and routing metadata for a graph visualisation.\n\nFields\n\npositions::Vector{Point2f}: Node positions in data coordinates\nkind::Symbol: One of :acyclic, :cyclic, :mixed_acyclic, or :mixed_cyclic\nnode_layers::Vector{Int}: Layer index for each node\ncomponent_index::Vector{Int}: Strongly connected component index for each node\ncomponents::Vector{Vector{Int}}: Node membership for each component\ncomponent_layers::Vector{Int}: Layer index for each component\nfeedback_edges::Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}: Directed edges routed as feedback edges\nedge_waypoints::Dict{Tuple{Int, Int}, Vector{Point2f}}: Extra waypoints for curved edges\nedge_curve_distances::Dict{Tuple{Int, Int}, Float64}: GraphMakie curve_distance per edge\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DAGLayoutResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.classify_graph_kind","page":"Layout & themes","text":"classify_graph_kind(g)\n\nClassify a graph by directed cyclicity and mixed-edge support.\n\nSelf-loops are ignored for this classification: they are drawn by GraphMakie as local self-arcs and should not force the multi-node cyclic / feedback layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.classify_graph_kind"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.feedback_edge_mask","page":"Layout & themes","text":"feedback_edge_mask(g, layout_result)\n\nReturn a Boolean mask, aligned with edges(g), indicating which directed edges should be rendered as curved feedback overlays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.feedback_edge_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.edge_waypoint_vector","page":"Layout & themes","text":"edge_waypoint_vector(g, layout_result)\n\nMaterialise a per-edge waypoint vector aligned with edges(g) for GraphMakie.graphplot!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.edge_waypoint_vector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.count_layered_crossings","page":"Layout & themes","text":"count_layered_crossings(g, layers)\n\nCount pairwise edge crossings between consecutive layers of a layered digraph. Used for layout quality checks; not a public drawing API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.count_layered_crossings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.compute_auto_label_aligns","page":"Layout & themes","text":"compute_auto_label_aligns(g::AbstractGraph, node_positions::AbstractVector)\n\nCompute optimal label alignment for each node to avoid edge overlaps.\n\nFor each node, finds the largest angular gap between incident edges and places the label in the middle of that gap. This ensures labels do not overlap with edges, improving readability of causal diagrams.\n\nArguments\n\ng::AbstractGraph: A graph from Graphs.jl (directed or undirected)\nnode_positions::AbstractVector: Vector of node positions (Point2f or similar)\n\nReturns\n\nVector{Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}}: Alignment tuples (:horizontal, :vertical) for each node, suitable for use with nlabels_align in GraphMakie.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nFor each node, collect angles of all incident edges (both incoming and outgoing)\nNormalise angles to [0, 2π] and sort\nFind the largest angular gap between adjacent edges (including wrap-around)\nPlace label in the middle of the largest gap\nMap the gap midpoint angle to one of 8 alignment directions\n\nEdge Cases\n\nIsolated nodes (no incident edges): Returns (:right, :bottom) as default\nSingle edge: Places label opposite the edge direction\nAll edges in one direction: Places label in the opposite hemisphere\n\nExamples\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\n# Compute positions (e.g., from a layout algorithm)\npositions = [Point2f(0, 0), Point2f(1, 0), Point2f(2, 0)]\n\n# Get optimal alignments\naligns = compute_auto_label_aligns(g, positions)\n# aligns[1] might be (:center, :top) since edge goes right\n# aligns[2] might be (:center, :top) since edges go left and right\n# aligns[3] might be (:center, :top) since edge goes left\n\nNotes\n\nWorks with any layout algorithm (uses computed node positions)\nHandles both directed and undirected graphs\nTime complexity: O(V × E) where V is vertices and E is edges\nIntended for outside-node labels: pair with a positive nlabels_distance (see DAGMakie.resolve_auto_align_label_settings). With distance 0 the non-centred alignments only shift text inside the marker and look broken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_auto_label_aligns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.align_to_direction","page":"Layout & themes","text":"align_to_direction(align::Tuple{Symbol, Symbol})\n\nConvert a Makie text-box align to the offset direction GraphMakie uses with nlabels_distance.\n\nMakie align names the edge of the label that sits on the anchor. Combined with a positive distance, that places the label on the opposite side of the node (e.g. (:left, :center) → offset east → label to the right of the node).\n\nArguments\n\nalign: Tuple of (:horizontal, :vertical) Makie text-align symbols\n\nReturns\n\nDirection from node toward the label (for use as distance .* dir)\n\nExamples\n\nalign_to_direction((:left, :center))   # (1, 0)  — label ends up east of node\nalign_to_direction((:right, :center))  # (-1, 0) — label ends up west of node\nalign_to_direction((:center, :bottom)) # (0, 1)  — label ends up north of node\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.align_to_direction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.estimate_label_extent","page":"Layout & themes","text":"estimate_label_extent(label, align, fontsize, distance)\n\nEstimate the extent of a label in pixel coordinates.\n\nReturns a named tuple (dx_min, dx_max, dy_min, dy_max) representing the approximate bounding box offset from the node position.\n\nArguments\n\nlabel::AbstractString: The label text\nalign::Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}: Alignment tuple (:horizontal, :vertical)\nfontsize::Real: Font size in pixels\ndistance::Real: Label distance from node in pixels\n\nReturns\n\nNamed tuple with dx_min, dx_max, dy_min, dy_max in pixels\n\nNotes\n\nUses approximate character width of 0.6 × fontsize for typical fonts\nReturns pixel-space estimates (caller must convert to data coordinates)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.estimate_label_extent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.estimate_label_pixel_size","page":"Layout & themes","text":"estimate_label_pixel_size(label, fontsize) -> (width, height)\n\nApproximate pixel width and height of label at fontsize (multiline aware). Uses the same 0.6 × fontsize character-width heuristic as estimate_label_extent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.estimate_label_pixel_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_size_for_inner_label","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_size_for_inner_label(label; fontsize, padding, min_size, marker=:auto)\n\nChoose a GraphMakie node_size (and marker) that encloses an in-node label.\n\nShort labels keep a circle whose diameter covers the text; wider labels keep FIT_NODE_MARKER (Makie.Circle) with a (width, height) size (an oval / ellipse). Pass marker = :circle or Circle to force a round disk; pass marker = :rect to force a rectangular box.\n\nReturns\n\n(size, marker) where size is a Real or (width, height) tuple\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_size_for_inner_label"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.fit_node_sizes_to_labels","page":"Layout & themes","text":"fit_node_sizes_to_labels(nlabels; fontsize, padding, min_size, markers=nothing)\n\nFit a size (and marker) for each in-node label.\n\nWhen markers === nothing, markers are FIT_NODE_MARKER (round or oval). When markers is a vector, sizes respect those shapes (:circle is normalised to FIT_NODE_MARKER so sizing matches the drawn glyph).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.fit_node_sizes_to_labels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.compute_label_bounds","page":"Layout & themes","text":"compute_label_bounds(node_positions, nlabels, nlabels_align, nlabels_distance, nlabels_fontsize)\n\nCompute the bounding box that encompasses all nodes and their labels.\n\nArguments\n\nnode_positions::AbstractVector: Vector of node positions (Point2f or similar)\nnlabels::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}: Vector of label strings\nnlabels_align: Vector of alignment tuples or single tuple for all\nnlabels_distance::Real: Label distance from node in pixels\nnlabels_fontsize::Real: Font size for labels\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max) in data coordinates\n\nNotes\n\nEstimates pixel-to-data conversion based on current data range\nAssumes typical figure width of ~500 pixels for the data range\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_label_bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.compute_padded_limits","page":"Layout & themes","text":"compute_padded_limits(node_positions, nlabels, nlabels_align, nlabels_distance, nlabels_fontsize; padding=0.1, node_sizes=nothing)\n\nCompute axis limits with padding that includes all nodes, labels, and marker extents.\n\nArguments\n\nnode_positions: Vector of node positions\nnlabels: Vector of label strings (or nothing for no labels)\nnlabels_align: Alignment specification\nnlabels_distance: Label distance in pixels\nnlabels_fontsize: Font size\npadding::Float64 = 0.1: Padding as fraction of range (0.1 = 10%)\nnode_sizes: Optional node marker size(s) in pixels; used so flat chains/columns are not cropped under DataAspect\n\nReturns\n\nTuple of tuples ((x_min, x_max), (y_min, y_max)) for axis limits\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_padded_limits"},{"category":"type","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.CurvedEdge","page":"Layout & themes","text":"CurvedEdge\n\nPer-edge bow for dagplot. Unspecified edges are straight.\n\nFields\n\nbow::Union{Real, Nothing}: Lateral quadratic control offset as a fraction of chord length (typical 0.12–0.35). Defaults to DEFAULT_EDGE_BOW. Ignored when distance is set.\ndistance::Union{Real, Nothing}: GraphMakie curve_distance in data units (perpendicular sagitta). The bend angle along the chord satisfies γ = 2 operatornameatan(2d  L) for chord length L.\nside::Symbol: :auto bows away from the nearest non-endpoint node on the chord; :left / :right are relative to the forward chord direction.\n\nExamples\n\nedge_routing = Dict(\n    (1, 4) => CurvedEdge(),              # default bow\n    (1, 5) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.28),    # deeper arc\n    (2, 4) => CurvedEdge(distance = 0.35),\n    (3, 5) => 0.18,                      # bow fraction shorthand\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.CurvedEdge"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_EDGE_BOW","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default lateral bow as a fraction of chord length for CurvedEdge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_EDGE_BOW"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.dag_theme","page":"Layout & themes","text":"dag_theme()\n\nReturn a clean theme for DAG visualisation.\n\nThis theme removes axes, grids, and frames to create publication-ready causal diagrams with focus on the graph structure.\n\nReturns\n\nTheme: A Makie theme with clean DAG styling\n\nUsage\n\nusing CairoMakie, DAGMakie\n\n# Apply theme globally\nset_theme!(dag_theme())\n\n# Or use with_theme for a single plot\nwith_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    dagplot(g, labels=[\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dag_theme"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.apply_dag_theme!","page":"Layout & themes","text":"apply_dag_theme!(ax)\n\nApply clean DAG theme to an existing axis.\n\nHides all axis decorations (spines, ticks, labels, grid) and sets DataAspect for proper graph proportions.\n\nArguments\n\nax: A Makie Axis object\n\nExample\n\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1])\napply_dag_theme!(ax)\ngraphplot!(ax, g)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.apply_dag_theme!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DAGStyle","page":"Layout & themes","text":"DAGStyle\n\nPreset styling configuration for DAG visualisation.\n\nFields\n\nnode_size::Real: Node size in pixels\nnode_color::Symbol: Default node colour\nnode_strokewidth::Real: Node outline width\nnode_strokecolor::Symbol: Node outline colour\nedge_color::Symbol: Edge colour\nedge_width::Real: Edge line width\narrow_size::Real: Arrowhead size\narrow_shift: Arrow position (:end or Float64)\nlabel_fontsize::Real: Label font size\nlabel_color::Symbol: Label colour\nlabel_distance::Real: Label distance from node\npadding::Float64: Padding fraction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DAGStyle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_style","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_style()\n\nReturn the default DAG styling configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_style"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.minimal_style","page":"Layout & themes","text":"minimal_style()\n\nReturn a minimal DAG style with smaller nodes and thinner edges (in-node labels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.minimal_style"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.bold_style","page":"Layout & themes","text":"bold_style()\n\nReturn a bold DAG style with larger nodes and thicker edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.bold_style"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.presentation_style","page":"Layout & themes","text":"presentation_style()\n\nReturn a style optimised for presentations (large, high contrast, in-node labels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.presentation_style"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.UNDIRECTED_EDGE_COLOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default colour for undirected (CPDAG skeleton) edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.UNDIRECTED_EDGE_COLOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_DISTANCE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Pixel offset used when label_position=:outer places labels outside nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_DISTANCE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_COLOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Label colour for outside-node labels (label_position=:outer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_COLOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default node size when label_position=:outer (compact markers; labels sit outside).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_INNER","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Within-layer node spacing when label_position=:inner (room for fitted markers / chords).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_INNER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_OUTER","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Within-layer node spacing when label_position=:outer (compact markers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_OUTER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_LABEL_PADDING","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Total padding (px) around text when fitting in-node markers to labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_LABEL_PADDING"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_MIN_SIZE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Minimum marker size (px) when fitting in-node markers to labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_MIN_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_RECT_ASPECT","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Width/height ratio above which fitted in-node markers become ovals (not disks).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_RECT_ASPECT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_MARKER","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Marker used when fitting node size to in-node labels.\n\nPrefer geometry Makie.Circle over the :circle BezierPath: Makie draws :circle at roughly 70% of markersize, so label-based sizes look too small and text overflows. Circle matches markersize one-to-one (including ovals via (width, height)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.FIT_NODE_MARKER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_COLOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default node colour (steel-blue; white labels read clearly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_COLOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_SIZE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default node size in pixels (large enough for in-node labels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_NODE_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_EDGE_COLOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default edge colour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_EDGE_COLOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_FONTSIZE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default label font size (compact outer labels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_FONTSIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_DISTANCE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default label distance from node (pixels); 0 centres labels in nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_DISTANCE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_POSITION","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default label placement (:outer = compact nodes + external labels).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_LABEL_POSITION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.DEFAULT_SELFEDGE_SIZE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Default GraphMakie selfedge_size (data units) for self-loops.\n\nGraphMakie's automatic size is half the nearest-neighbour distance, which is oversized on typical DAG layouts; keep loops compact beside the marker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DEFAULT_SELFEDGE_SIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_COLOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Colour for pedagogical modifier annotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_COLOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_STYLE","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Line style for pedagogical modifier annotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_STYLE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_WIDTH","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Line width for pedagogical modifier annotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.MODIFIER_EDGE_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_CONFOUNDER","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Highlight colour for confounders / exogenous noise / instruments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_CONFOUNDER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_MEDIATOR","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Highlight colour for mediators or other focal nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_MEDIATOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_EFFECT","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Fill for IDAG effect-measure nodes (same family as mediators).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_EFFECT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_SWIG_FIXED","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Fill for the fixed half of a SWIG split node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NODE_COLOR_SWIG_FIXED"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.TREATMENT_STROKEWIDTH","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Stroke width for treatment / exposure nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.TREATMENT_STROKEWIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.OUTCOME_STROKEWIDTH","page":"Layout & themes","text":"Stroke width for outcome nodes (emphasised outline).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.OUTCOME_STROKEWIDTH"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_node_color","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_node_color(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_node_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_node_marker","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_node_marker(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default marker for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_node_marker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_node_strokewidth","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_node_strokewidth(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default stroke width for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_node_strokewidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_node_strokecolor","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_node_strokecolor(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default outline colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_node_strokecolor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.default_node_label_color","page":"Layout & themes","text":"default_node_label_color(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default in-node label colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.default_node_label_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_type_marker","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_type_marker(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the marker shape for a given node type (delegates to default_node_marker).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_type_marker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_type_color","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_type_color(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the default fill colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_type_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_type_strokewidth","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_type_strokewidth(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the stroke width for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_type_strokewidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_type_strokecolor","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_type_strokecolor(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the stroke colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_type_strokecolor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.node_type_label_color","page":"Layout & themes","text":"node_type_label_color(type::NodeType)\n\nReturn the in-node label colour for a given node type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node_type_label_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.apply_node_type_styling","page":"Layout & themes","text":"apply_node_type_styling(node_types::Vector{NodeType})\n\nGenerate styling arrays for a list of node types.\n\nArguments\n\nnode_types: Vector of NodeType for each node\n\nReturns\n\nNamed tuple with:\ncolors: Vector of fill colours\nmarkers: Vector of marker shapes\nstrokewidths: Vector of stroke widths\nstrokecolors: Vector of stroke colours\nlabel_colors: Vector of in-node label colours\n\n\n\n\n\napply_node_type_styling(spec::DAGSpec)\n\nGenerate styling arrays from a DAGSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.apply_node_type_styling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.typed_confounding_graph","page":"Layout & themes","text":"typed_confounding_graph()\n\nCreate a confounding graph with proper node types.\n\nReturns DAGSpec with: Confounder → Treatment → Outcome, Confounder → Outcome\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.typed_confounding_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.typed_mediation_graph","page":"Layout & themes","text":"typed_mediation_graph()\n\nCreate a mediation graph with proper node types.\n\nReturns DAGSpec with: Treatment → Mediator → Outcome, Treatment → Outcome\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.typed_mediation_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.typed_instrumental_graph","page":"Layout & themes","text":"typed_instrumental_graph()\n\nCreate an instrumental variable graph with proper node types.\n\nReturns DAGSpec with: Instrument → Treatment → Outcome, Confounder → Treatment, Confounder → Outcome\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.typed_instrumental_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/layout_and_themes/#DAGMakie.typed_collider_graph","page":"Layout & themes","text":"typed_collider_graph()\n\nCreate a collider graph with proper node types.\n\nReturns DAGSpec with: Cause₁ → Collider ← Cause₂\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.typed_collider_graph"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plotting/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plotting/#Main-plotting-functions","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Main plotting functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plotting/#Convenience-pattern-functions","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Convenience pattern functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plotting/#Edge-labels-and-terminal-markers","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Edge labels and terminal markers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plotting/#Visual-grammar-(interactions-/-DiD)","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Visual grammar (interactions / DiD)"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot(spec::DAGSpec; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG from a DAGSpec specification.\n\nArguments\n\nspec::DAGSpec: A DAG specification with graph, nodes, and edges\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nSame preferred kwargs as dagplot / dagplot! (labels, label_position, color_by, …)\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (fig, ax, p)\n\n\n\n\n\ndagplot(g; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a clean DAG visualisation with publication-ready defaults.\n\nThis is a convenience wrapper that creates a new figure and axis, then delegates to dagplot!. All keyword arguments are passed through.\n\nArguments\n\ng::AbstractGraph: A graph from Graphs.jl to plot\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nfigure_size::Tuple{Int, Int} = (600, 400): Figure dimensions\nPreferred plot kwargs (full list on dagplot!): labels, label_position (:inner / :outer), color_by, exposure, outcome, label_obstacle_graph, plus GraphMakie pass-throughs (nlabels_*, elabels_*, …).\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (fig, ax, p) where:\nfig: The Figure object\nax: The Axis object\np: The GraphPlot object (for accessing node positions, etc.)\n\nExamples\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Simple chain graph\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g; labels=[\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nsave(\"dag.png\", fig)\n\n# Outer labels\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g; labels=[\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"], label_position=:outer)\n\n# Access node positions\npositions = p[:node_pos][]\n\n\n\n\n\ndagplot(mg::MixedGraph; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a DAG visualisation with both directed and bidirected edges.\n\nBidirected edges (↔) are rendered as curved arcs with double arrowheads, representing unmeasured common causes (latent confounders).\n\nAdditional Keyword Arguments\n\nbidirected_color = :gray: Colour for bidirected edges\nbidirected_width = 1.0: Line width for bidirected edges\nbidirected_style = :dash: Line style for bidirected edges\nbidirected_curvature = 0.3: Curvature of bidirected arcs\nbidirected_arrow_size = 8: Size of arrowheads on bidirected edges\n\nExamples\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Confounded treatment-outcome\nmg = mixed_graph(2, [(1, 2)], [(1, 2)])  # X → Y with X ↔ Y\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg, labels=[\"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n# Instrumental variable with confounding\nmg = mixed_graph(3, [(1, 2), (2, 3)], [(2, 3)])  # Z → X → Y, X ↔ Y\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg, labels=[\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot!","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot!(ax, spec::DAGSpec; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG from a DAGSpec specification into an existing axis.\n\nKwargs match dagplot! on AbstractGraph.\n\n\n\n\n\ndagplot!(ax, g; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG into an existing axis with clean, publication-ready styling.\n\nAutomatically hides axis decorations, applies DataAspect, and sets appropriate limits to prevent clipping of nodes and labels.\n\nArguments\n\nax: A Makie Axis object\ng::AbstractGraph: A graph from Graphs.jl to plot\n\nLayout Keyword Arguments\n\nlayout_mode = :auto: :auto uses a deterministic layered layout for DAGs and an SCC-aware layout for cyclic digraphs; :acyclic / :cyclic force those modes; :spring uses NetworkLayout Spring(). Pass explicit layout = … (positions or a NetworkLayout algorithm) to bypass the strategy\npadding::Float64 = 0.1: Padding around graph as fraction of range\nlayer_gap = 2.6: Spacing between layered columns (or rows)\nnode_gap = nothing: Within-layer spacing. Defaults to DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_INNER (2.6) when label_position=:inner, and DEFAULT_NODE_GAP_OUTER (1.8) when label_position=:outer\nComparison / intervention helpers reuse a shared DAGLayoutResult so panels keep the same node positions\n\nNode Keyword Arguments\n\nnode_size = DEFAULT_NODE_SIZE: Node marker size in pixels (theme default 34; outer labels use OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE unless set explicitly)\nnode_color = DEFAULT_NODE_COLOR: Node fill colour (single value or vector; steel-blue by default)\nnode_strokewidth = 1.0: Node outline width\nnode_strokecolor = :black: Node outline colour\nnode_marker = :circle: Node marker shape\n\nEdge Keyword Arguments\n\nedge_color = :black: Edge colour\nedge_width = 1.0: Edge line width\nstraight_edges = nothing: Shorthand for edge_routing = Dict(e => :straight for e in edges)\nedge_routing = nothing: Dict((src, dst) => spec) per-edge routing — straight by default; curve with :curved, CurvedEdge (default bow DEFAULT_EDGE_BOW), a bow fraction (Real), or CurvedEdge(distance=…) for GraphMakie bend angle γ = 2operatornameatan(2dL)\nlong_edge_routing = :quadratic: Geometry for bowed edges (:none, :natural_cubic, …)\narrow_size = 10: Arrowhead size\narrow_shift = :end: Arrow position (:end or Float64 0-1)\nedge_weights = nothing: Optional signed edge values. A negative value uses an inhibitory -| terminal; pass either one value per Graphs.edges(g) entry or a structural matrix B with B[i, j] the value on j → i.\nelabels = nothing: Edge labels in Graphs.edges(g) order. Accepts plain Strings or Makie LaTeXStrings (e.g. from structural_edge_labels with latex=true) for structural parameters or short mechanism TeX on edges\nelabels_fontsize, elabels_distance, elabels_rotation, elabels_side, … : forwarded to GraphMakie (use elabels_rotation = 0 to keep maths upright)\nselfedge_size, selfedge_direction, selfedge_width: GraphMakie self-loop geometry. When the graph has self-loops and selfedge_size is omitted, DAGMakie defaults to DEFAULT_SELFEDGE_SIZE (compact beside the node)\n\nLabel Keyword Arguments\n\nlabels = nothing: Node labels (preferred DAGMakie name). nlabels= is the GraphMakie-compatible alias\nnlabels_align = (:center, :center): Makie text-box anchor (or vector of anchors). Named sides are edges of the label, not “label goes this side of the node”: (:left, :center) left-anchors the text so it sits to the right of the node. See the Label Alignment guide.\nlabel_position = :outer: :outer (default) places labels outside in the largest angular gap (compact OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE markers, dark text); :inner centres labels in the node fill (white text; pass fit_node_size_to_labels=true for oval markers on long names) label_position = :outer. true still enables outer placement when label_position is left at :inner\nlabel_obstacle_graph = nothing: Optional graph used only for outer-label angle gaps (defaults to the plotted graph). Intervention plots pass the factual DAG so grey removed parent edges still count as obstacles.\nfit_node_size_to_labels = false: When true and label_position=:inner, size in-node markers from each label (short labels stay round; wider labels become ovals). Skipped for outer labels or an explicit node_size. Pass true with label_position=:inner for fitted in-node markers.\nnlabels_distance = 0: Label distance from node in pixels along nlabels_align (0 centres labels in nodes; with (:center, :center) the offset is zero regardless of distance—use a non-centred align or label_position = :outer for outside labels)\nnlabels_fontsize = 16: Label font size\nnlabels_color = :white: Label colour (white on dark node fills; dark via OUTER_LABEL_COLOR when label_position = :outer)\n\nColouring / dagitty roles\n\ncolor_by = nothing: Set :ancestors (or true) for dagitty-style ancestor colours, or :adjustment to also emphasise a backdoor adjustment set (needs CausalInference or adjustment=). Deprecated alias: smart\nexposure, outcome: Exposure and outcome node indices (required when color_by is on). - adjustment: Optional Set{Int} for color_by=:adjustment\n\nAdditional Arguments\n\nAdditional keyword arguments are passed to GraphMakie.graphplot!\n\nReturns\n\nThe GraphPlot object\n\nExamples\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\n# Simple usage\nfig = Figure()\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1])\ndagplot!(ax, g, labels=[\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n# With node colours indicating roles\ndagplot!(ax, g,\n    labels=[\"Confounder\", \"Treatment\", \"Outcome\"],\n    node_color=[NODE_COLOR_CONFOUNDER, DEFAULT_NODE_COLOR, DEFAULT_NODE_COLOR]\n)\n\n# Multiple DAGs in one figure\nfig = Figure(size=(1200, 400))\nax1, ax2, ax3 = Axis(fig[1, 1]), Axis(fig[1, 2]), Axis(fig[1, 3])\ndagplot!(ax1, g1, labels=[\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"])\ndagplot!(ax2, g2, labels=[\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ndagplot!(ax3, g3, labels=[\"P\", \"Q\", \"R\"])\n\n\n\n\n\ndagplot!(ax, mg::MixedGraph; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a MixedGraph (with bidirected edges) into an existing axis.\n\nAccepts the same preferred label kwargs as dagplot! on AbstractGraph (labels, label_position, …), plus bidirected-edge styling (bidirected_color, bidirected_width, …).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_chain","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_chain(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a simple chain DAG: X₁ → X₂ → ... → Xₙ\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels for each node in the chain\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (fig, ax, p)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_chain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_fork","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_fork(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a fork DAG: X₁ ← X₂ → X₃\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [left, fork, right]\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_fork"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_collider","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_collider(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a collider DAG: X₁ → X₂ ← X₃\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [left, collider, right]\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_collider"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_confounding","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_confounding(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a confounding DAG: Z → X → Y, Z → Y.\n\nUses a triangle layout by default (confounder on top, treatment and outcome below). Layered left-to-right layout places one node per layer and draws the backdoor edge through the treatment node; pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [confounder, treatment, outcome]\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_confounding"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_mediation","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_mediation(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a mediation DAG: X → M → Y, X → Y.\n\nUses a triangle layout by default (mediator on top, treatment and outcome below). Layered left-to-right layout places one node per layer and draws the direct effect through the mediator; pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [treatment, mediator, outcome]\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_mediation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_confounded","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_confounded(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a simple confounded graph: X → Y with X ↔ Y.\n\nUses a horizontal layout by default so the bidirected arc is visible above the directed edge; pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [treatment, outcome]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_confounded"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_frontdoor","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_frontdoor(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a frontdoor criterion graph: X → M → Y with X ↔ Y.\n\nUses a triangle layout by default (mediator on top). Layered layout collinearises the three nodes and obscures the bidirected confounding arc; pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [treatment, mediator, outcome]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_frontdoor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_iv_confounded","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_iv_confounded(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot an IV graph with confounding: Z → X → Y, X ↔ Y.\n\nUses a triangle layout by default (instrument on top). Layered layout collinearises the chain and hides the bidirected confounding arc; pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{String}: Labels [instrument, treatment, outcome]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_iv_confounded"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_m_bias","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_m_bias(labels; kwargs...)\n\nPlot the classic five-node M-bias DAG with explicit latents:\n\nU₁ → X, U₁ → M, U₂ → M, U₂ → Y\n\nDefault layout forms the letter M (latents on top, X–M–Y on the base). Pass layout to override.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels::Vector{<:AbstractString}: Labels U₁ U₂ X M Y\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_m_bias"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.structural_edge_labels","page":"Plotting","text":"structural_edge_labels(g, B; latex=true, digits=2, ensure_self_loops=true)\n\nBuild GraphMakie elabels for g from a structural parameter matrix B (linear SEM / linear SCM weights).\n\nConvention: B[i, j] is the structural weight of node j in the assignment for node i, i.e. the parameter on the directed edge j → i (including self-loops when i == j). Labels are returned in Graphs.edges(g) order (the order GraphMakie expects for elabels).\n\nWhen ensure_self_loops=true (default), non-zero diagonal entries that are missing from g are added in place via ensure_structural_self_loops! before labels are built. Off-diagonal non-zeros without a matching edge still warn (and are omitted from labels). You can also build the full edge set with graph_from_structural_matrix.\n\nThis is deliberately not named “effects”: in the Pearl ladder an effect is usually an interventional or counterfactual estimand, whereas these labels are structural parameters (or other short mechanism annotations) on the graph.\n\nWhen latex=true (default), each entry is a Makie LaTeXString so parameters render as maths on the edge. Set latex=false for plain String labels.\n\nExamples\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nB = [0 0 0; 0.8 0 0; 0.5 1.2 3.0]  # diagonal 3.0 → self-loop on Y\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n    labels = labels,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g, B),  # adds Y → Y automatically\n    elabels_fontsize = 14,\n    elabels_distance = 12,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n)\n\n\n\n\n\nstructural_edge_labels(g, labels; latex=false)\n\nPass edge annotations already ordered as Graphs.edges(g). With latex=true, wrap plain strings (or numbers) as LaTeXString via Makie.latexstring so short TeX such as \"\\\\beta_{ZX}\" or a fragment of a structural assignment renders on the edge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.structural_edge_labels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.edge_coefficient_labels","page":"Plotting","text":"edge_coefficient_labels(args...; kwargs...)\n\nDeprecated alias for structural_edge_labels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.edge_coefficient_labels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.edge_terminal_markers","page":"Plotting","text":"edge_terminal_markers(g, weights; atol=0.0)\n\nReturn GraphMakie arrow markers in Graphs.edges(g) order. Negative entries in weights use INHIBITORY_EDGE_MARKER, producing an inhibitory -| terminal; non-negative entries use the ordinary arrowhead.\n\nweights may be a vector with one entry per edge, or a square structural matrix B whose B[i, j] entry is the weight on j → i. This is display metadata only: DAGMakie does not infer a mechanism sign from graph topology. Values with absolute magnitude at most atol use an ordinary arrowhead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.edge_terminal_markers"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.INHIBITORY_EDGE_MARKER","page":"Plotting","text":"Marker for an inhibitory edge terminal (a bar perpendicular to the edge).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.INHIBITORY_EDGE_MARKER"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.modifier_edge","page":"Plotting","text":"modifier_edge(src, dst; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a pedagogical modifier EdgeSpec (dash-dot, dark gray).\n\nModifier edges annotate that one variable modifies an effect; they are not inputs to d-separation. Prefer an IDAG (DAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_idag_spec) when the claim is about effect variation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.modifier_edge"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_side_by_side","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_side_by_side(left, right; titles, figure_size, kwargs...)\n\nPlot two DAGSpec (or graph) panels with shared keyword overrides.\n\nUsed for outcome-DAG | IDAG and factual-DAG | SWIG companion figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_side_by_side"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.did_2x2_factual_spec","page":"Plotting","text":"did_2x2_factual_spec(; labels)\n\nTime-expanded factual DAG for a two-group, two-period DiD design.\n\nNodes (default order): G, U, Y₀, Y₁, A₁.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.did_2x2_factual_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.did_2x2_swig_spec","page":"Plotting","text":"did_2x2_swig_spec(; labels)\n\nSWIG under do(A₁ = 0) for the untreated world used by parallel trends.\n\nNodes (default order): G, U, Y₀, Y₁(0), A₁, a=0. Incoming edges stay on the random half A₁; outflows leave from the fixed half a=0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.did_2x2_swig_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.did_2x2_factual_layout","page":"Plotting","text":"did_2x2_factual_layout()\n\nManual positions for the factual 2×2 DiD DAG (time left→right).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.did_2x2_factual_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.did_2x2_swig_layout","page":"Plotting","text":"did_2x2_swig_layout()\n\nManual positions for the DiD SWIG with a split treatment node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.did_2x2_swig_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plotting/#DAGMakie.dagplot_did_swig","page":"Plotting","text":"dagplot_did_swig(; kwargs...)\n\nSide-by-side factual 2×2 DiD DAG and untreated-world SWIG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_did_swig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"Public symbols in DAGMakie are organised across five reference pages:\n\nPlotting — dagplot, dagplot!, convenience pattern plotters, edge labels/terminals, and visual-grammar (IDAG / DiD SWIG) helpers.\nSpecs & graphs — NodeType, EdgeType, DAGSpec, MixedGraph, graph constructors, and graph utilities.\nHighlighting & interventions — paths, HighlightSpec, dagitty-style color_by role colouring, display-only do_surgery, and CausalQuery.\nTemporal & skeletons — time-indexed and mixed temporal layouts, temporal role propagation, and CPDAG skeletons.\nLayout & themes — layered/SCC layouts, label alignment and sizing, edge routing, themes, and node-type styling.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Preferred-keyword-names","page":"Overview","text":"Public plotting prefers discoverable names; legacy aliases still work:\n\nPrefer Alias (removed from public API)\nlabels nlabels (GraphMakie pass-through still accepted)\nlabel_position = :outer former auto_align_labels=true\nlabel_obstacle_graph former auto_align_graph\ncolor_by former smart\nexposure former treatment (colouring kwargs)\nshow_removed_edges former show_original\ndo_node_labels former relabel_nodes\nOUTER_LABEL_* former AUTO_ALIGN_*\n\nGraphMakie pass-throughs keep the nlabels_* / elabels_* prefixes.","title":"Preferred keyword names"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#How-DAGMakie-compares","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"DAGMakie.jl draws causal DAGs, mixed graphs, and related diagrams with Makie. It is visualisation only: identification and estimation stay in CausalDynamics.jl / CausalInference.jl and CausalTargeted.jl.\n\nClosest R tools are ggdag and dagitty plots. In Python, people typically glue networkx, matplotlib, graphviz, or CausalGraphicalModels.\n\nChoose DAGMakie when you want layered causal themes, bidirected confounding, path highlighting, time-indexed layouts, or SWIG / DiD visual grammar in the same Makie stack as SciML and book figures.\n\nPrefer ggdag / dagitty when your analysis is already tidyverse-centric, you need the dagitty web GUI, or a one-off ggplot is enough.\n\nStack overview: ECOSYSTEM_COMPARISON.md.\n\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph juliaStack [Julia_typed_pipeline]\n    G[Graph_CDM] --> ID[identify_certificate]\n    ID --> SIM[simulate_counterfactual]\n    ID --> EST[LMTP_mediation]\n    ID --> PLOT[DAGMakie]\n    SIM --> EST\n  end","title":"How DAGMakie compares"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Legend","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Mark Meaning\nYes First-class, documented\nPartial Possible with glue or a limited API\n— Not in that package’s usual scope\nUnique Strong differentiator here","title":"Legend"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Versus-R-and-Python-(DAG-visualisation)","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Capability DAGMakie R Python\nLayered causal DAG plot Yes Yes (ggdag, dagitty) Partial (networkx / graphviz / CGMs)\nBidirected confounding arcs Yes Yes (ggdag / dagitty) Partial\nPath / adjustment / d-sep highlight Yes (± CausalInference) Yes (ggdag + dagitty) Partial (DoWhy / CGMs)\nSmart / dagitty-like ancestor colouring Yes (color_by=:ancestors) Yes (dagitty) Partial\nDisplay-only do(·) comparison figures Yes Partial (custom) Partial (custom)\nTime-indexed / unrolled layouts Yes Partial (custom ggplot) Partial (custom)\nSWIG / DiD / IDAG visual grammar Unique Partial (custom) Partial (custom)\nSame Makie stack as SciML figures Unique — —\nGraph GUI / interactive editor — Yes (dagitty web) Partial","title":"Versus R and Python (DAG visualisation)"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Julia-neighbours","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Package Role\nGraphMakie.jl General graph plotting backend\nCausalDynamics.jl Identification sets and TemporalUnrolling consumed by plot helpers (comparison)\nCausalTargeted.jl Continuous MTP / LMTP estimation (not plotted here) (comparison)\nCausalMediation.jl Interventional, natural, organic, and controlled direct mediation (not plotted here) (comparison)","title":"Julia neighbours"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#What-is-distinctive-here","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Causal diagram defaults — steel-blue themes, node roles, bidirected geometry\nVisual grammar — IDAG effect-measure nodes and DiD SWIG layouts (guide)\nPipeline fit — figures sit next to SciML and Documenter @example chunks without leaving Julia","title":"What is distinctive here"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#What-is-deliberately-absent","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Identification algorithms, estimation, and a dagitty-style GUI. Pass sets into dagplot_*, or load CausalInference / CausalDynamics when the plot should compute highlighting. See Causal analysis.\n\nThe CDCS book uses these figures in narrative context.","title":"What is deliberately absent"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Visual-grammar:-interactions-and-DiD-SWIGs","page":"Visual grammar","text":"DAGMakie keeps a small, fixed visual vocabulary for effect modification / interaction figures and difference-in-differences (DiD) single-world intervention graphs (SWIGs). dag_theme sets the canvas and axis chrome (white background, hidden spines/ticks/grids, and DataAspect), while DAGSpec node types and default_style set fill, marker, stroke, and label colours: in-node labels, steel-blue observed/treatment/outcome fills, goldenrod confounders, seagreen mediators / effect nodes, and gray hollow latents.","title":"Visual grammar: interactions and DiD SWIGs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Terms","page":"Visual grammar","text":"SWIG (single-world intervention graph; Richardson & Robins, 2013, arXiv:1201.5658): under do(A = a), the intervened node is drawn as a split — a random half A that still receives incoming edges, and a fixed half a from which outgoing edges leave. Counterfactual labels such as Y(0) live on this graph; the factual DAG alone does not carry them.\nIDAG (interaction DAG): companion to an ordinary outcome DAG in which the outcome node is replaced by an effect-measure node (e.g. δ). Use it when the question is how an effect varies with a modifier, not when identifying EY mid do(A).\n\nnote: Display only\nModifier edges, effect-measure nodes, and SWIG fixed halves are pedagogical annotations. They do not change d-separation or identification. Use CausalInference.jl / CausalDynamics.jl for those queries.","title":"Terms"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#House-rule","page":"Visual grammar","text":"Identification / adjustment → ordinary outcome DAG (steel-blue / goldenrod).\n“Does G change the effect of A?” → IDAG companion (green effect-measure node), or a dash-dot mod edge with an explicit caption.\n“Is DiD justified?” → time-expanded factual DAG plus a SWIG for the untreated world (parallel trends lives on Y_t(0), not on the factual DAG alone).","title":"House rule"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Node-conventions","page":"Visual grammar","text":"Role NodeType Fill / stroke Marker\nObserved / default Observed :steelblue, stroke 1 circle\nTreatment Treatment :steelblue, stroke 2.5 circle\nOutcome Outcome :steelblue, stroke 2.0 darkgray circle\nConfounder / context Confounder :goldenrod circle\nMediator Mediator :seagreen circle\nLatent Latent gray / hollow, stroke 2 circle\nEffect measure (IDAG) EffectMeasure :seagreen rect\nSWIG fixed half SwigFixed white, stroke 2, black label rect\n\nTreatment and outcome stay in the steel-blue family so default / minimal / bold / presentation themes keep working; roles are stroke and shape, not a new rainbow of fills.","title":"Node conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Edge-conventions","page":"Visual grammar","text":"Kind Style Notes\nCausal  solid black usual GraphMakie arrows; an explicitly declared negative edge_weights value uses an inhibitory ``-\nLatent confounding  dashed curve MixedGraph\nRemoved by do() dashed, light intervention plots\nModifier annotation dash-dot, :darkgray modifier_edge; caption required\n\nusing DAGMakie\n\ne = modifier_edge(1, 3)\n(e.type, e.style, e.color, e.label)\n(Modifier, :dashdot, :darkgray, \"mod\")","title":"Edge conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Example-1-—-Vaccine-nutrition","page":"Visual grammar","text":"Nutrition N confounds vaccination V and outcome Y, and may also modify the vaccine effect on an additive scale. The left panel is the outcome DAG for identification; the right panel is an IDAG where Y is replaced by an effect-measure node δ (Nilsson et al. style).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    DAGMakie.dagplot_vaccine_nutrition_interaction()\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nConstructors if you need the specs separately:\n\nDAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_outcome_spec\nDAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_idag_spec\nDAGMakie.dagplot_vaccine_nutrition_interaction\n\nCaption pattern: Left: structural DAG for identifying EY mid do(V) after adjusting for N. Right: IDAG for additive effect modification; the effect node is not an outcome random variable.","title":"Example 1 — Vaccine × nutrition"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Example-2-—-Canonical-22-DiD-SWIG","page":"Visual grammar","text":"Two groups G, two periods, treatment A_1 only for the treated group in period 1, with unit-level latent U. The left panel is the factual time-expanded DAG; the right panel is a SWIG under do(A_1 = 0) (split node as above). Incoming edges stay on the random half A_1; outflows leave from the fixed half a=0, and the post-period outcome is labelled Y_1(0).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    dagplot_did_swig()\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nConstructors:\n\ndid_2x2_factual_spec / did_2x2_factual_layout\ndid_2x2_swig_spec / did_2x2_swig_layout\ndagplot_did_swig\n\nCaption pattern: Left: two-period DAG with unit-level U. Right: SWIG for the untreated world; parallel trends is a statement about Y_t(0), read on the SWIG.\n\nDo not draw two-way fixed-effect dummies as causal nodes; show substantive latents (here U) instead.","title":"Example 2 — Canonical 2×2 DiD SWIG"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/visual_grammar/#Side-by-side-companions","page":"Visual grammar","text":"dagplot_side_by_side is the shared layout for outcome | IDAG and factual | SWIG pairs (same habit as dagplot_do_comparison).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nleft = DAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_outcome_spec()\nright = DAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_idag_spec()\nfig = with_theme(dag_theme()) do\n    dagplot_side_by_side(\n        left, right;\n        titles = (\"Outcome DAG\", \"IDAG\"),\n        layout = DAGMakie.vaccine_nutrition_layout(),\n    )\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Side-by-side companions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#Skeletons-and-time-indexed-plots","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"Two layout helpers sit beside ordinary dagplot: undirected skeletons (CPDAG / PC-style output) and time-indexed grids for graphs unrolled over occasions.","title":"Skeletons and time-indexed plots"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#Undirected-skeletons","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"PC and related algorithms often return an undirected edge as a pair of opposing arrows. Plotting both looks like a cycle; collapse them first with digraph_skeleton, or use dagplot_skeleton so arrowheads stay off and edges use UNDIRECTED_EDGE_COLOR.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Opposing arcs encode an undirected edge between X and Y; Z → X is directed.\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # X → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 1)  # Y → X  (undirected {X,Y})\nadd_edge!(g, 3, 1)  # Z → X\n\nlabels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"]\n# Z above X so reciprocal X–Y arcs stay clear of Z.\nlayout = Point2f[Point2f(-1, 0), Point2f(1, 0), Point2f(-1, 1.2)]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 280))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Directed (misleading)\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Skeleton\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g; labels = labels, layout = layout)\ndagplot!(ax2, digraph_skeleton(g); labels = labels, layout = layout)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nOne-liner when you only need the skeleton figure:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_skeleton(g; labels = labels, layout = layout)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nPassing an undirected SimpleGraph to dagplot / dagplot! also suppresses arrowheads and applies the undirected edge colour.","title":"Undirected skeletons"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#Relational-graphs","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"CausalDynamics can ground a declared relational schema and skeleton to an ordinary directed graph whose node labels retain (entity type, instance, attribute) provenance. Plot that grounded graph with dagplot and pass those labels explicitly. DAGMakie does not infer relational mechanisms, identify effects across unseen skeletons, or decide whether a relation is causal; future plate/grouping helpers remain display-only.","title":"Relational graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#Time-indexed-unrolling","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"For a graph with n_variables × n_times nodes in CausalDynamics order (outer loop over occasions, inner loop over variables), use dagplot_time_indexed. Columns run left→right in time; rows are variables.\n\nWith color_by = :ancestors (or :ancestors_temporal), exposure and outcome roles propagate across each variable row so later occasions of the same variable keep treatment / outcome / ancestor colours (#5). Pass scalar node indices or (variable_index, time_index) tuples for exposure and outcome.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# W, A, Y × two occasions (CausalDynamics unroll order).\ng = SimpleDiGraph(6)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # W₁ → A₁\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # W₁ → Y₁\nadd_edge!(g, 4, 5)  # W₂ → A₂\nadd_edge!(g, 4, 6)  # W₂ → Y₂\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 5)  # A₁ → A₂\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 6)  # A₁ → Y₂\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_time_indexed(\n    g, 3, 2;\n    labels = [\"W₁\", \"A₁\", \"Y₁\", \"W₂\", \"A₂\", \"Y₂\"],\n    color_by = :ancestors,\n    exposure = (2, 1),\n    outcome = (3, 2),\n    figure_size = (640, 260),\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFor manual styling without the plot wrapper, use apply_node_type_styling or temporal_role_styling.","title":"Time-indexed unrolling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#Mixed-temporal-layout-and-glyphs","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"When an unrolled graph reuses a single node for a from-onset attribute as well as pointwise state nodes, use dagplot_temporal with the graph's explicit node keys. A pointwise key is (variable, time); a reused node is (variable, nothing) and is placed at its onset time. Markers follow value_representation: only :interval_summary uses the rounded rectangle under the package convention. Attributes, trajectories, and from-onset supports remain circles unless you pass an explicit node_marker. Shape therefore never encodes enduring identity or node count. Show referent identity with labelled lanes or grouping, separately from causal arrows.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nkeys = [(:diagnosis, 0), (:pasture, nothing), (:weight, 0), (:weight, 1)]\ng = SimpleDiGraph(4)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # diagnosis[0] → pasture\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # diagnosis[0] → weight[0]\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 4)  # pasture → weight[1]\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_temporal(\n    g,\n    keys;\n    onset_times = [0, 1, 0, 0],  # placement of the single-node key (:pasture, nothing)\n    value_representations = [:state, :attribute, :state, :state],\n    labels = [\"diagnosis[0]\", \"pasture\", \"weight[0]\", \"weight[1]\"],\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSpacing keywords dx and dy stretch columns and rows. For a TemporalUnrolling from CausalDynamics.jl, prefer dagplot_temporal(unrolling) after using DAGMakie (labels via temporal_node_label; markers from value_representation / CausalDynamics extension).\n\nThe display layer does not infer temporal provenance. When an auditable distinction is needed, obtain records from CausalDynamics with temporal_edge_records(unrolling) and inspect the role field. In CausalDynamics, declared relation_kind (:causal_influence, :constitutive_persistence, :constitutive_dependence, :participation) is the sole input to causal_projection; construction temporal_edge_role labels which supports a causal edge joins: :onset_assignment forms a single-node attribute from a pointwise node, :recurrent_influence reuses a single-node attribute at later times, :pointwise_influence connects pointwise nodes, and :causal_influence connects two single-node keys (non-causal declared relation_kind values are returned verbatim). Passing these records to plotting code is display metadata; it must not create alias vertices, turn an :onset_assignment edge into a constitutive constraint, or change the graph used for identification. Prefer marker_for_value_representation when representation is known. do_surgery on process/semantic graph_kind requires allow_non_causal=true or an explicit causal projection.","title":"Mixed temporal layout and glyphs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/skeletons_and_time/#See-also","page":"Skeletons & time","text":"Visual Grammar — DiD SWIGs and interaction IDAGs\nNode Types & Styling — EffectMeasure / SwigFixed colours\nBasic Plotting — layout_mode and general dagplot options","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#Highlighting-and-interventions","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"","title":"Highlighting & interventions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#Paths","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"","title":"Paths"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#Highlighting","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"","title":"Highlighting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#Smart-/-dagitty-colouring","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"","title":"Smart / dagitty colouring"},{"category":"section","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#Interventions","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"","title":"Interventions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.CausalPath","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"CausalPath\n\nA path through a DAG, tracking nodes and edge directions (forward or backward along graph edges).\n\nFields\n\nnodes::Vector{Int}: Sequence of nodes\ndirections::Vector{Symbol}: Direction of each edge (:forward or :backward)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.CausalPath"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.PathSegment","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"PathSegment\n\nA segment of a path, tracking the direction of traversal.\n\nFields\n\nnode::Int: The node in this segment\ndirection::Symbol: :forward (→), :backward (←), or :start\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.PathSegment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.path_edges","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"path_edges(path::CausalPath)\n\nReturn the edges in a path as (src, dst) tuples in graph orientation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.path_edges"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.is_directed_path","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"is_directed_path(path::CausalPath)\n\nReturn true if every edge in the path is forward (a directed causal path).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.is_directed_path"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.HighlightSpec","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"HighlightSpec\n\nSpecification for highlighting elements in a DAG plot.\n\nFields\n\nnodes::Vector{Int}: Nodes to highlight\nnode_colors::Vector: Colors for highlighted nodes\nedges::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}: Edges to highlight (as src, dst pairs)\nedge_colors::Vector: Colors for highlighted edges\nlabels::Vector{String}: Optional labels for highlighted nodes\n\nPrecedence\n\nWhen the same node or edge is listed more than once, the first listing wins (exposure / outcome / adjustment colours should therefore precede path colours in merged specs). Default theme colours sit underneath.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.HighlightSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.highlight_from_path","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"highlight_from_path(path::CausalPath; color=:red)\n\nCreate a HighlightSpec from a causal path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.highlight_from_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.highlight_from_paths","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"highlight_from_paths(paths::Vector{CausalPath}; colors=nothing)\n\nCreate a HighlightSpec from multiple paths with different colors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.highlight_from_paths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.highlight_adjustment_set","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"highlight_adjustment_set(g::AbstractGraph, treatment::Int, outcome::Int, \n                         adjustment::Set{Int}; treatment_color=:green, \n                         outcome_color=:blue, adjustment_color=:orange)\n\nCreate a HighlightSpec for visualising an adjustment set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.highlight_adjustment_set"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.highlight_backdoor_paths","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"highlight_backdoor_paths(paths::Vector{CausalPath};\n                         blocked_color=:gray, open_color=:red,\n                         blocked::AbstractVector{Bool}=Bool[])\n\nCreate a HighlightSpec from precomputed backdoor paths.\n\nPass blocked of the same length as paths to colour blocked vs open paths. If blocked is empty, all paths use open_color.\n\nPath finding belongs in CausalInference.jl / CausalDynamics.jl — not DAGMakie.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.highlight_backdoor_paths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_highlighted","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_highlighted(g::AbstractGraph, highlight::HighlightSpec; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a DAG plot with highlighted elements.\n\nArguments\n\ng: The graph\nhighlight: HighlightSpec defining what to highlight\nkwargs...: Additional arguments passed to dagplot\n\nExamples\n\ng = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])[1]\npath = CausalPath([1, 2, 3]; directions = [:forward, :forward])\nhighlight = highlight_from_path(path, color=:red)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_highlighted(g, highlight, labels=[\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_highlighted"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_highlighted!","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_highlighted!(ax, g::AbstractGraph, highlight::HighlightSpec; kwargs...)\n\nPlot a highlighted DAG into an existing axis.\n\nPrefer labels= and label_position= (see dagplot!). Alias nlabels= / auto_align_labels= still work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_highlighted!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_backdoor","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_backdoor(g, treatment, outcome; adjustment=Set{Int}(), paths=CausalPath[], kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG highlighting treatment, outcome, an adjustment set, and optional precomputed backdoor paths.\n\nFor automatic adjustment / d-separation from CausalInference.jl, load that package so DAGMakieCausalInferenceExt activates, or pass adjustment / paths explicitly from CausalDynamics.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_backdoor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_dsep","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_dsep(g, x, y, z; separated, kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG with X, Y, and conditioning set Z highlighted.\n\nPass separated::Bool for the axis title (from CausalInference.jl dsep or CausalDynamics.jl d_separated). With using CausalInference, separated defaults via the package extension when omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_dsep"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_causal_paths","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_causal_paths(g, treatment, outcome; paths, kwargs...)\n\nHighlight precomputed directed paths from treatment to outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_causal_paths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_adjustment","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_adjustment(g, treatment, outcome; adjustment=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG with an adjustment set. Pass adjustment::Set{Int} explicitly, or using CausalInference so the extension can compute a minimal backdoor set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_adjustment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SmartNodeRole","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"SmartNodeRole\n\nStructural role of a node relative to a chosen exposure and outcome (dagitty- style ancestor highlighting).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SmartNodeRole"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.classify_smart_roles","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"classify_smart_roles(g, exposure, outcome; anc_treatment=nothing, anc_outcome=nothing)\n\nClassify each vertex into a SmartNodeRole given exposure and outcome (1-based indices). Argument names treatment / anc_treatment remain for compatibility with older call sites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.classify_smart_roles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.smart_style_for_graph","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"smart_style_for_graph(g, exposure, outcome; mode=:ancestors, adjustment=nothing)\n\nReturn a named tuple of per-node styling for dagitty-like colouring (color_by modes).\n\nModes\n\n:ancestors — exposure / outcome / ancestor-of-X / ancestor-of-Y / both / gray\n:adjustment — same, plus thicker stroke on a backdoor adjustment set (requires CausalInference, or pass adjustment::Set{Int})\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.smart_style_for_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.apply_smart_kwargs","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"apply_smart_kwargs(g; color_by, exposure, outcome, adjustment, kwargs...)\n\nIf color_by is enabled, merge dagitty-style colours into keyword args for dagplot!. Explicit node_color from the caller wins.\n\nDeprecated aliases: smart= for color_by=, treatment= for exposure=.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.apply_smart_kwargs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_smart","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_smart(g, treatment, outcome; color_by=:ancestors, kwargs...)\n\nConvenience wrapper: dagplot with dagitty-style ancestor colours. Prefer color_by=; smart= remains an alias.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_smart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.ancestor_sets","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"ancestor_sets(g, exposure, outcome) -> (anc_exposure, anc_outcome)\n\nReturn ancestor sets of exposure and outcome. Uses CausalInference.jl when the extension is loaded; otherwise Graphs reverse-BFS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.ancestor_sets"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.ancestors_via_graphs","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"ancestors_via_graphs(g, seeds) -> Set{Int}\n\nAncestors of seeds including the seeds themselves, via reverse BFS on inneighbors (Graphs only; no CausalInference).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.ancestors_via_graphs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.smart_node_color","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"smart_node_color(role::SmartNodeRole)\n\nReturn the default fill colour for a smart node role.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.smart_node_color"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.smart_label_color","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"smart_label_color(role::SmartNodeRole)\n\nReturn in-node label colour for a smart role (white on role fills; black on light gray irrelevant nodes). With label_position=:outer (or legacy auto_align_labels=true), apply_smart_kwargs replaces these with OUTER_LABEL_COLOR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.smart_label_color"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_EXPOSURE","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Fill for the exposure / treatment node under smart colouring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_EXPOSURE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_OUTCOME","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Fill for the outcome node under smart colouring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_OUTCOME"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_EXPOSURE","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Dagitty-like fill for ancestors of the exposure only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_EXPOSURE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_OUTCOME","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Dagitty-like fill for ancestors of the outcome only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_OUTCOME"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_BOTH","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Dagitty-like fill for ancestors of both (often backdoor-relevant).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_ANC_BOTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_IRRELEVANT","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Fill for nodes outside the ancestral closure of exposure ∪ outcome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.SMART_COLOR_IRRELEVANT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.Intervention","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"Intervention\n\nSpecification for an intervention (do(·)) on one or more variables.\n\nFields\n\nnodes::Vector{Int}: Nodes being intervened on\nvalues::Vector{String}: Display values for the interventions (e.g., \"x=1\")\nlabel::String: Overall intervention label (e.g., \"do(X=1)\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.Intervention"},{"category":"type","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.CausalQuery","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"CausalQuery\n\nA query about a causal effect.\n\nFields\n\ntreatment::Int: Treatment variable\noutcome::Int: Outcome variable  \nintervention::Union{Intervention, Nothing}: Intervention (if specified)\nconditioning::Set{Int}: Variables to condition on\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.CausalQuery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.do_surgery","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"do_surgery(g::AbstractGraph, intervention_nodes::Vector{Int})\n\nPerform display-only graph surgery for do(·): remove all incoming edges to intervention nodes. Use this to draw mutilated DAGs in Makie; it is not an identification API.\n\nFor CausalDynamics identification and CDM interventions, use DoIntervention / apply_intervention (and related helpers) in CausalDynamics.jl.\n\nReturns a graph where intervened nodes no longer have parents (for plotting).\n\nArguments\n\ng: Original DAG\nintervention_nodes: Nodes being intervened on (set to fixed values)\n\nReturns\n\nNew SimpleDiGraph with incoming edges to intervention nodes removed\n\nExamples\n\n# Confounding: Z → X → Y, Z → Y\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # Z → X\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # X → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # Z → Y\n\n# do(X) - intervene on X\ng_do = do_surgery(g, [2])\n# Result: X → Y, Z → Y (Z → X edge removed)\n\n\n\n\n\ndo_surgery(g::AbstractGraph, intervention_node::Int; kwargs...)\n\nSingle-node intervention convenience method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.do_surgery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.do_surgery!","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"do_surgery!(g::SimpleDiGraph, intervention_nodes::Vector{Int})\n\nIn-place display-only graph surgery — modifies the original graph. Same boundary as do_surgery: for plotting mutilated DAGs, not CausalDynamics ID.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.do_surgery!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_intervention","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_intervention(g::AbstractGraph, intervention::Intervention;\n                     show_removed_edges::Bool=true, kwargs...)\n\nPlot a DAG with intervention applied, showing removed edges.\n\nArguments\n\ng: Original DAG\nintervention: Intervention specification\nshow_removed_edges: If true, overlay severed parent edges as dashed grey (deprecated alias: show_original)\nkwargs...: Additional arguments for dagplot\n\nExamples\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nint = Intervention(2)  # do(X)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_intervention(g, int, labels=labels)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_intervention"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_intervention!","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_intervention!(ax, g::AbstractGraph, intervention::Intervention; kwargs...)\n\nPlot intervention into an existing axis.\n\nPreferred kwargs (see also dagplot!): labels, label_position, show_removed_edges (alias show_original), do_node_labels (alias relabel_nodes). Outer labels use the factual DAG as label_obstacle_graph when removed edges are overlaid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_intervention!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_do","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_do(g::AbstractGraph, intervention_node::Int; labels=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nConvenience function for single-node intervention visualisation.\n\nPrefer labels= (alias nlabels=). Other kwargs match dagplot_intervention / dagplot!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_do"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_comparison","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_comparison(g::AbstractGraph, intervention::Intervention;\n                   labels=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nCreate a side-by-side comparison of original and post-intervention DAGs.\n\nBoth panels share node positions and axis limits, so corresponding nodes remain aligned at the same scale. Prefer labels= (alias nlabels=). Shared layout and remaining kwargs are passed to dagplot! / dagplot_intervention!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.dagplot_do_comparison","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"dagplot_do_comparison(g::AbstractGraph, intervention_node::Int;\n                      labels=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nConvenience function for side-by-side comparison with single intervention.\n\nPrefer labels= (alias nlabels=). See dagplot_comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.dagplot_do_comparison"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.intervention_label","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"intervention_label(var_name::String; value=nothing)\n\nCreate a formatted intervention label: do(X) or do(X=x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.intervention_label"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.format_intervention_labels","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"format_intervention_labels(nlabels::Vector{String}, intervention::Intervention)\n\nUpdate node labels to show intervention notation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.format_intervention_labels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/highlighting_and_do/#DAGMakie.query_to_string","page":"Highlighting & interventions","text":"query_to_string(query::CausalQuery, nlabels::Vector{String})\n\nConvert a causal query to readable notation for plot titles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.query_to_string"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/interventions/#Interventions-(do(·))","page":"Interventions","text":"Graph surgery and intervention plots live in DAGMakie. Display follows Pearl’s do(·) graph surgery (Pearl, 2009). do_surgery is display-only (mutilate a DAG for figures); it is not an identification API.","title":"Interventions (do(·))"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/interventions/#Boundary-with-CausalDynamics.jl","page":"Interventions","text":"DAGMakie reuses Pearl names (Intervention, CausalQuery, do_surgery) for diagram rendering only. For whether an effect is identifiable, and for executable SCM/CDM interventions, use CausalInference.jl or CausalDynamics.jl (DoIntervention, apply_intervention, causal_projection). When calling do_surgery with a process or semantic graph_kind, DAGMakie refuses surgery unless you pass an explicit causal projection or set allow_non_causal = true.","title":"Boundary with CausalDynamics.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/interventions/#Single-node-intervention-and-removed-edges","page":"Interventions","text":"By default (show_removed_edges = true), dagplot_intervention and dagplot_do overlay severed incoming arrows in dashed light grey. When label_position = :outer, labels align against the factual graph (label_obstacle_graph) so those grey removed parent edges still count as obstacles.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n\n# do(X): remove incoming edges to X\ng_do = do_surgery(g, 2)\n@assert !has_edge(g_do, 1, 2)\n@assert has_edge(g_do, 2, 3)\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_intervention(g, Intervention(2); labels = labels)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nPass show_removed_edges = false to hide the severed arrows, or do_node_labels = true to rewrite the intervened node’s label to do(X) (by default do_node_labels = false keeps the node label \"X\" intact and puts do(X) in the axis title).","title":"Single-node intervention and removed edges"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/interventions/#Side-by-side-comparison-and-CausalQuery","page":"Interventions","text":"dagplot_do_comparison (and dagplot_comparison) place the factual and intervened DAGs side by side using a shared DAGLayoutResult and linked axes so node positions and scale match across panels. CausalQuery and query_to_string format interventional or conditional distributions for plot titles:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nq = CausalQuery(2, 3; intervention = Intervention(2))\n@assert query_to_string(q, labels) == \"P(Y | do(X))\"\n\nfig = dagplot_do_comparison(g, 2; labels = labels)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Side-by-side comparison and CausalQuery"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Choosing-a-diagram","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"This page maps common biological study designs and causal questions to the right diagram type and DAGMakie helper. DAGMakie is visualisation-only: it draws causal diagrams and highlights paths or adjustment sets that you supply (or compute via CausalDynamics.jl or CausalInference.jl).","title":"Choosing a diagram"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Where-this-package-fits","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"Task Typical question Package\nDraw assumptions How do I communicate my causal assumptions clearly to colleagues and reviewers? DAGMakie.jl (this package)\nIdentify & simulate Which covariates must I adjust for (or exclude)? CausalDynamics.jl\nEstimate effects What is the causal effect of shifting an exposure or treatment? CausalTargeted.jl\nDecompose pathways How much of an effect acts directly versus through an intermediate mechanism? CausalMediation.jl","title":"Where this package fits"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Reading-a-causal-diagram","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"In a directed acyclic graph (DAG), nodes represent variables (observed or unmeasured) and directed arrows (A to Y) represent direct causal influences (Pearl, 2009; Hernán & Robins, 2020). What is missing from a DAG is as important as what is drawn: omitting an arrow asserts that one variable does not directly cause another.\n\nDAGMakie encodes node and edge roles consistently so readers can scan a figure without decoding ad-hoc colours (Textor et al., 2016; Cinelli, Forney & Pearl, 2024):\n\nSolid nodes represent observed variables; dashed or hollow nodes represent latent (unmeasured) variables.\nSolid arrows (to) represent causal effects; inhibitory terminals (-vert) represent declared negative effects (edge_weights).\nDashed bidirected arcs (leftrightarrow) represent an unmeasured common cause of two variables (MixedGraph).\nRole colouring (color_by) distinguishes exposure, outcome, confounders, mediators, and adjustment sets.","title":"Reading a causal diagram"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Match-your-biological-question-to-a-diagram","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"Study design or biological question Diagram type Entry point in DAGMakie Guide / reference\nThree-variable building blocks (confounder, mediator, collider, chain) Canonical motif DAG dagplot_confounding, dagplot_mediation, dagplot_fork, dagplot_chain, dagplot_collider Pearl (2009); Cinelli, Forney & Pearl (2024). Getting Started\nShow which covariates to adjust for (and which backdoor paths are blocked) Highlighted backdoor / adjustment DAG dagplot_backdoor, dagplot_adjustment, dagplot_smart Textor et al. (2016). Causal Analysis\nUnmeasured confounding (e.g. unmeasured genetics or habitat quality affecting both A and Y) Mixed graph with bidirected arc (leftrightarrow), frontdoor, IV, or M-bias MixedGraph, dagplot_confounded, dagplot_frontdoor, dagplot_iv_confounded, dagplot_m_bias Pearl (2009). Bidirected Edges\nRepeated measurements, cohorts, or ecological time series Time-indexed unrolled DAG (variables on rows, time on columns) dagplot_temporal, dagplot_time_indexed Hernán & Robins (2020). Skeletons & Time\nFeedback loops or reciprocal physiology (e.g. host–parasite or endocrine feedback) Cyclic graph (SCC-aware layout) or time-unrolled DAG dagplot on a cyclic DiGraph, or dagplot_temporal Basic Plotting\nExperimental manipulation or do(·) intervention Graph surgery (removing incoming arrows to the intervened node) dagplot_do, dagplot_do_comparison, do_surgery Pearl (2009). Interventions\nEffect modification / interaction or difference-in-differences (DiD) Interaction DAG (IDAG) or Single-World Intervention Graph (SWIG) modifier_edge, dagplot_did_swig, dagplot_side_by_side Richardson & Robins (2013). Visual Grammar\nDiscovery output (undirected skeleton / CPDAG) Skeleton with undirected and directed edges dagplot_skeleton Skeletons & Time\nQuantitative path diagram (showing estimated effects or structural coefficients on arrows) Edge-labelled DAG elabels + structural_edge_labels or edge_coefficient_labels Getting Started","title":"Match your biological question to a diagram"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Minimal-example:-good-control-vs-interventional-surgery","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"A common biological pattern is baseline confounding (W = age/season affecting parasite load A and survival Y). Drawing the observational DAG alongside the do(A) surgery graph makes clear why an observational analysis must adjust for W whereas a randomised experiment breaks W to A:\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng, _ = confounding_graph([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig = dagplot_do_comparison(\n    g, 2;\n    labels = [\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"],\n    titles = (\"Observational (adjust W)\", \"Experimental do(A)\"),\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Minimal example: good control vs interventional surgery"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#References","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"Cinelli, C., Forney, A., & Pearl, J. (2024). A crash course in good and bad controls. Sociological Methods & Research, 53(3), 1071–1104. doi:10.1177/00491241221099552\nHernán, M. A., & Robins, J. M. (2020). Causal Inference: What If. Chapman & Hall/CRC.\nPearl, J. (2009). Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press.\nRichardson, T. S., & Robins, J. M. (2013). Single world intervention graphs (SWIGs): A unification of the counterfactual and graphical approaches to causality. arXiv:1201.5658\nTextor, J., van der Zander, B., Gilthorpe, M. S., Liśkiewicz, M., & Ellison, G. T. H. (2016). Robust causal inference using directed acyclic graphs: the R package 'dagitty'. International Journal of Epidemiology, 45(6), 1887–1894. doi:10.1093/ije/dyw341","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Next-steps","page":"Choosing a diagram","text":"Executable motif and styling examples: Getting Started.\nRole colouring and adjustment highlighting: Causal Analysis.\nTime-indexed grids and interval glyphs: Skeletons & Time.\nVisual grammar for interactions and DiD SWIGs: Visual Grammar.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Specs-and-graphs","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Specs & graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Types","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Node-types","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Node types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Edge-types","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Edge types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAG-specification","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"DAG specification"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Mixed-graphs","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Mixed graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Graph-construction","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Graph construction"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Mixed-graph-operations","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Mixed graph operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#Utilities","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"","title":"Utilities"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.NodeType","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"NodeType\n\nEnumeration of node types in causal diagrams.\n\nValues\n\nObserved: Measured variable (filled circle)\nLatent: Unmeasured variable (hollow circle or dashed outline)\nTreatment: Treatment/exposure variable (thick stroke)\nOutcome: Outcome variable (emphasised stroke)\nInstrument: Instrumental variable\nConfounder: Confounding variable\nMediator: Mediating variable\nCollider: Collider variable\nEffectMeasure: Causal-effect node on an IDAG (not a factual outcome)\nSwigFixed: Fixed half of a SWIG split node (intervened value)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NodeType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.NodeSpec","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"NodeSpec\n\nSpecification for a single node in a DAG.\n\nFields\n\nlabel::String: Display label for the node\ntype::NodeType: Type of node (determines styling)\ncolor: Override colour (nothing = use type default)\nsize::Union{Real, Tuple, Nothing}: Override size (nothing = use default; a (width, height) tuple is passed through to Makie for non-square markers)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.NodeSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.node","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"node(label; kwargs...)\n\nShorthand for creating a NodeSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.node"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.EdgeType","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"EdgeType\n\nEnumeration of edge types in causal diagrams.\n\nValues\n\nDirected: Standard directed edge (→)\nBidirected: Bidirected edge for unmeasured confounding (↔)\nUndirected: Undirected edge (—)\nModifier: Pedagogical annotation that one variable modifies an effect (dash-dot; not an input to d-separation)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.EdgeType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.EdgeSpec","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"EdgeSpec\n\nSpecification for a single edge in a DAG.\n\nFields\n\nsrc::Int: Source node index\ndst::Int: Destination node index\ntype::EdgeType: Type of edge\ncolor: Override colour\nwidth::Union{Real, Nothing}: Override line width\nstyle::Union{Symbol, Nothing}: Line style (:solid, :dash, :dot, etc.)\nlabel::Union{String, Nothing}: Optional edge label\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.EdgeSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.edge","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"edge(src, dst; kwargs...)\n\nShorthand for creating an EdgeSpec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.edge"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.DAGSpec","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"DAGSpec\n\nComplete specification for a causal DAG visualisation.\n\nFields\n\ngraph::AbstractGraph: The underlying graph structure\nnodes::Vector{NodeSpec}: Node specifications (one per vertex)\nedges::Vector{EdgeSpec}: Edge specifications (overrides for specific edges)\ntitle::Union{String, Nothing}: Optional title for the DAG\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DAGSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.MixedGraph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"MixedGraph\n\nA graph that supports both directed edges (→) and bidirected edges (↔).\n\nBidirected edges represent unmeasured common causes (latent confounders) in causal diagrams. Internally, bidirected edges are stored separately from the directed graph structure.\n\nFields\n\ndirected::SimpleDiGraph: The directed edges\nbidirected::Set{Tuple{Int, Int}}: Set of bidirected edge pairs (unordered)\n\nExamples\n\n# Create a mixed graph with confounding\nmg = MixedGraph(3)\nadd_directed_edge!(mg, 1, 3)  # X → Y\nadd_directed_edge!(mg, 2, 3)  # Z → Y\nadd_bidirected_edge!(mg, 1, 2)  # X ↔ Z (unmeasured confounder)\n\n# Or from existing directed graph\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\nmg = MixedGraph(g, [(1, 2)])  # Add bidirected edge\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.MixedGraph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.chain_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"chain_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a simple chain DAG: X₁ → X₂ → ... → Xₙ\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of node labels\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.chain_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.fork_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"fork_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a fork DAG: X₁ ← X₂ → X₃ (with X₂ as the fork point)\n\nAssumes 3 nodes: labels[1] ← labels[2] → labels[3]\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 node labels\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.fork_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.collider_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"collider_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a collider DAG: X₁ → X₂ ← X₃ (with X₂ as the collider)\n\nAssumes 3 nodes: labels[1] → labels[2] ← labels[3]\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 node labels\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.collider_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.confounding_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"confounding_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a classic confounding DAG: Z → X → Y, Z → Y\n\nAssumes 3 nodes: Z (confounder), X (treatment), Y (outcome)\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 node labels [Z, X, Y]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.confounding_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.mediation_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"mediation_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a mediation DAG: X → M → Y, X → Y\n\nAssumes 3 nodes: X (treatment), M (mediator), Y (outcome)\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 node labels [X, M, Y]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.mediation_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.instrumental_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"instrumental_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate an instrumental variable DAG: Z → X → Y, U → X, U → Y\n\nAssumes 4 nodes: Z (instrument), X (treatment), Y (outcome), U (unobserved confounder)\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 4 node labels [Z, X, Y, U]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (g, labels) where g is the graph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.instrumental_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.mixed_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"mixed_graph(n::Int, directed_edges, bidirected_edges)\n\nCreate a MixedGraph with specified directed and bidirected edges.\n\nArguments\n\nn::Int: Number of vertices\ndirected_edges: Iterable of (src, dst) pairs for directed edges\nbidirected_edges: Iterable of (i, j) pairs for bidirected edges\n\nExamples\n\n# Confounded treatment-outcome graph\nmg = mixed_graph(2, [(1, 2)], [(1, 2)])  # X → Y with X ↔ Y\n\n# Instrumental variable setup\nmg = mixed_graph(4,\n    [(1, 2), (2, 3)],  # Z → X → Y\n    [(2, 3)]           # X ↔ Y (unmeasured confounding)\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.mixed_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.confounded_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"confounded_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a simple confounded graph: X → Y with X ↔ Y (unmeasured confounder).\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 2 labels [treatment, outcome]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (mg, labels) where mg is a MixedGraph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.confounded_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.frontdoor_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"frontdoor_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate a frontdoor criterion graph: X → M → Y with X ↔ Y.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 labels [treatment, mediator, outcome]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (mg, labels) where mg is a MixedGraph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.frontdoor_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.iv_confounded_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"iv_confounded_graph(labels::Vector{String})\n\nCreate an instrumental variable graph with confounding: Z → X → Y, X ↔ Y.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 3 labels [instrument, treatment, outcome]\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (mg, labels) where mg is a MixedGraph\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.iv_confounded_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.m_bias_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"m_bias_graph(labels)\n\nCreate the classic five-node M-bias DAG with explicit latents:\n\nU₁ → X, U₁ → M, U₂ → M, U₂ → Y\n\nNo directed edge X  Y (pure M-bias). Conditioning on the collider M opens the non-causal path X  U₁  M  U₂  Y.\n\nArguments\n\nlabels: Vector of 5 labels U₁ U₂ X M Y (defaults to those symbols)\n\nReturns\n\nTuple (mg, labels) where mg is a MixedGraph with no bidirected edges (latents are drawn explicitly rather than as X  M  Y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.m_bias_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.m_bias_spec","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"m_bias_spec(labels)\n\nDAGSpec for the five-node M-bias DAG with latent / collider styling.\n\nSame edges as m_bias_graph: U₁  X, U₁  M, U₂  M, U₂  Y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.m_bias_spec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.graph_from_structural_matrix","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"graph_from_structural_matrix(B; atol=0.0)\n\nBuild a SimpleDiGraph from a structural parameter matrix B (linear SEM / linear SCM weights), including self-loops for non-zero diagonal entries.\n\nConvention matches structural_edge_labels: B[i, j] is the weight of node j in the assignment for node i, so a non-zero entry yields the directed edge j → i. In particular, B[i, i] ≠ 0 yields a self-loop at i, which GraphMakie draws as a self-pointing arc.\n\nEntries with absolute value at most atol are treated as absent.\n\nArguments\n\nB: Square structural weight matrix\natol: Absolute tolerance for treating entries as zero\n\nReturns\n\nSimpleDiGraph with one edge per non-zero structural weight\n\nExample\n\nB = [0.0 0.0 0.0; 0.8 0.0 0.0; 0.5 1.2 3.0]\ng = graph_from_structural_matrix(B)  # edges 1→2, 1→3, 2→3, and self-loop 3→3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.graph_from_structural_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.ensure_structural_self_loops!","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"ensure_structural_self_loops!(g, B; atol=0.0)\n\nAdd missing self-loops i → i whenever |B[i, i]| > atol. Mutates g and returns it. Off-diagonal structure is left unchanged.\n\nUsed by structural_edge_labels when ensure_self_loops=true (the default) so a non-zero diagonal is enough to draw self-pointing arcs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.ensure_structural_self_loops!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.add_directed_edge!","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"add_directed_edge!(mg::MixedGraph, i::Int, j::Int)\n\nAdd a directed edge i → j to the mixed graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.add_directed_edge!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.add_bidirected_edge!","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"add_bidirected_edge!(mg::MixedGraph, i::Int, j::Int)\n\nAdd a bidirected edge i ↔ j to the mixed graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.add_bidirected_edge!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.has_bidirected_edge","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"has_bidirected_edge(mg::MixedGraph, i::Int, j::Int)\n\nCheck if there is a bidirected edge between nodes i and j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.has_bidirected_edge"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.bidirected_edges","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"bidirected_edges(mg::MixedGraph)\n\nReturn an iterator over all bidirected edge pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.bidirected_edges"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.num_bidirected_edges","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"num_bidirected_edges(mg::MixedGraph)\n\nReturn the number of bidirected edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.num_bidirected_edges"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.compute_bidirected_path","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"compute_bidirected_path(p1::Point2f, p2::Point2f; curvature=0.3)\n\nCompute a curved path for a bidirected edge between two points.\n\nReturns a vector of points representing a quadratic Bézier curve that arcs above the straight line connecting the two points.\n\nArguments\n\np1: Start point\np2: End point\ncurvature::Float64 = 0.3: How much the arc curves (fraction of distance)\n\nReturns\n\nVector of Point2f representing the curved path\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_bidirected_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.compute_all_bidirected_paths","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"compute_all_bidirected_paths(mg::MixedGraph, positions::AbstractVector; curvature=0.3)\n\nCompute curved paths for all bidirected edges in a mixed graph.\n\nArguments\n\nmg: The mixed graph\npositions: Vector of node positions\ncurvature: Arc curvature parameter\n\nReturns\n\nVector of path vectors, one per bidirected edge\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.compute_all_bidirected_paths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.bidirected_arrow_positions","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"bidirected_arrow_positions(mg::MixedGraph, positions::AbstractVector; curvature=0.3, arrow_offset=0.1)\n\nCompute positions and rotations for arrowheads on bidirected edges.\n\nReturns positions and rotation angles for arrows at both ends of each bidirected edge.\n\nArguments\n\nmg: The mixed graph\npositions: Vector of node positions\ncurvature: Arc curvature parameter\narrow_offset: How far from the node centre to place arrows (0-0.5)\n\nReturns\n\nNamed tuple with:\npositions: Vector of Point2f for arrow positions\nrotations: Vector of Float64 rotation angles\nedge_indices: Which bidirected edge each arrow belongs to\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.bidirected_arrow_positions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.is_dag","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"is_dag(g::AbstractGraph)\n\nCheck if a directed graph is acyclic (a valid DAG).\n\nArguments\n\ng: A directed graph\n\nReturns\n\ntrue if the graph has no cycles, false otherwise\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.is_dag"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.edge_list","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"edge_list(g::AbstractGraph)\n\nReturn a vector of (src, dst) tuples for all edges in the graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.edge_list"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.adjacency_to_graph","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"adjacency_to_graph(adj::AbstractMatrix)\n\nCreate a DiGraph from an adjacency matrix.\n\nArguments\n\nadj: Square adjacency matrix where adj[i,j] != 0 indicates edge i → j\n\nReturns\n\nSimpleDiGraph with edges from the adjacency matrix\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.adjacency_to_graph"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.graph_from_edges","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"graph_from_edges(n::Int, edge_pairs::Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}})\n\nCreate a DiGraph from a list of edge pairs.\n\nArguments\n\nn: Number of vertices\nedge_pairs: Vector of (src, dst) tuples\n\nReturns\n\nSimpleDiGraph with the specified edges\n\nExample\n\ng = graph_from_edges(3, [(1, 2), (2, 3), (1, 3)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.graph_from_edges"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.get_node_positions","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"get_node_positions(p)\n\nExtract node positions from a GraphPlot object.\n\nArguments\n\np: A GraphMakie GraphPlot object\n\nReturns\n\nVector of node positions (Point2f or Point3f)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.get_node_positions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/specs_and_graphs/#DAGMakie.graph_extent","page":"Specs & graphs","text":"graph_extent(node_positions)\n\nCompute the spatial extent of a graph from node positions.\n\nArguments\n\nnode_positions: Vector of node positions\n\nReturns\n\nNamed tuple (x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, x_range, y_range)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.graph_extent"},{"category":"section","location":"#DAGMakie.jl","page":"Home","text":"DAGMakie draws causal diagrams for observational studies, experiments, and time-evolving biological systems using Makie, making confounders, mediators, unmeasured variables, interventions, and temporal structure explicit before fitting a statistical model.\n\nDeciding which diagram fits your study? See Choosing a diagram to match your biological question or study design to the right graph type and helper.\n\nDAGMakie plots directed acyclic, directed cyclic, and mixed causal graphs with Makie, building on GraphMakie.jl. Defaults omit axes and grids; node types follow common causal-diagram conventions from Pearl (2009) and related practice (observed, latent, treatment, outcome), with helpers for bidirected confounding, path highlighting (Textor et al., 2016, dagitty-style roles), display-only do(·) surgery, and a visual grammar for interaction IDAGs and DiD SWIGs. Example layouts often follow Cinelli, Forney & Pearl (2024, doi:10.1177/00491241221099552). Temporal glyphs, single-node placement, and display-only surgery are summarised in Terminology; boundaries and validation checks live in Scope & boundaries (BOUNDARIES.md). DOIs and BibTeX keys: References.","title":"DAGMakie.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Compared-with-R-and-Python","page":"Home","text":"DAGMakie brings layered causal DAGs, bidirected confounding arcs, dagitty-style path/role highlighting, time-indexed layouts, and SWIG/DiD visual grammar into Julia’s Makie stack—alongside SciML, CausalDynamics.jl, CausalTargeted.jl, and CausalMediation.jl. Choose DAGMakie when causal figures should live in Julia and Documenter; prefer ggdag / dagitty for tidyverse pipelines or the dagitty web GUI. See Ecosystem comparison and ECOSYSTEM_COMPARISON.md.","title":"Compared with R and Python"},{"category":"section","location":"#Capabilities","page":"Home","text":"Layouts are deterministic for DAGs (layered) and SCC-aware for cyclic graphs. Edges are straight by default; mark curved edges with :curved, CurvedEdge(), or a bow fraction to receive quadratic Bézier samples (long_edge_routing = :quadratic) (Basic Plotting). Undirected skeletons and time-indexed grids cover CPDAG output and unrolled temporal DAGs (Skeletons & Time). Themes (default, minimal, bold, presentation) control stroke weight and spacing. Node labels prefer labels=; default label_position = :outer (compact markers, external auto-aligned text). Use :inner with fit_node_size_to_labels=true for in-node white labels. Optional color_by role colouring; on time-indexed plots, roles propagate across variable rows (see Skeletons & Time). Convenience constructors cover chain, fork, collider, confounding, and mediation patterns. Edge labels (elabels, structural_edge_labels) carry structural path coefficients or short LaTeX mechanism fragments (Getting Started). Path helpers accept adjustment sets directly, or load CausalInference / CausalDynamics when identification should be computed at plot time.","title":"Capabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"DAGMakie\")\n\nDAGMakie is on the Julia General registry. It is visualisation-only; identification and do(·) calculus stay in CausalInference.jl or CausalDynamics.jl.\n\nOr for development:\n\nPkg.develop(path=\"path/to/DAGMakie.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-example","page":"Home","text":"using DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Confounding DAG Z → X → Y, Z → Y (triangle layout: confounder on top)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_confounding([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation","page":"Home","text":"Guides: Choosing a diagram · Getting Started · Basic plotting · Node types & styling · Visual grammar · Skeletons & time · Bidirected edges · Causal highlighting · Interventions\nAPI Reference: Overview · Plotting · Specs & graphs · Highlighting & interventions · Temporal & skeletons · Layout & themes\nArchitecture & Validation: Scope & boundaries · Terminology · Ecosystem comparison · References\n\nPages = [\n    \"orientation.md\",\n    \"getting-started.md\",\n    \"guide/basic.md\",\n    \"guide/styling.md\",\n    \"guide/visual_grammar.md\",\n    \"guide/skeletons_and_time.md\",\n    \"guide/bidirected.md\",\n    \"guide/causal.md\",\n    \"guide/interventions.md\",\n    \"api.md\",\n    \"scope.md\",\n    \"terminology.md\",\n    \"comparison.md\",\n    \"references.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"module","location":"#DAGMakie.DAGMakie","page":"Home","text":"DAGMakie\n\nPlot directed acyclic and related causal graphs with Makie, building on GraphMakie.jl. Defaults omit axes and grids; helpers cover common causal diagram conventions, bidirected confounding, and display-only do(·) surgery.\n\nIdentification (d-separation, adjustment sets) belongs in CausalInference.jl; executable SCMs belong in CausalDynamics.jl. Optional convenience plots that call CausalInference activate via using CausalInference.\n\nLayouts are deterministic for DAGs; themes (dag_theme, …) control stroke and spacing. Pattern constructors include chain, fork, collider, and confounding. Prefer discoverable plot kwargs: labels, label_position, color_by, exposure, show_removed_edges (legacy aliases still work).\n\nQuick Start\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # Z → X\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # Z → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # X → Y\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g, labels=[\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nsave(\"confounding_dag.png\", fig)\n\nMain Functions\n\ndagplot(g; kwargs...) - Create a new figure with DAG plot\ndagplot!(ax, g; kwargs...) - Plot DAG into existing axis\ncompute_auto_label_aligns(g, positions) - Compute optimal label positions\ndag_theme() - Return the default Makie theme for DAG figures\n\nConvenience Patterns\n\ndagplot_chain(labels) - X₁ → X₂ → ... → Xₙ\ndagplot_fork(labels) - X ← Y → Z\ndagplot_collider(labels) - X → Y ← Z\ndagplot_confounding(labels) - Z → X → Y, Z → Y\ndagplot_mediation(labels) - X → M → Y, X → Y\n\nSee the documentation for full details and examples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DAGMakie.DAGMakie"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Scope-and-design-boundaries","page":"Scope & boundaries","text":"DAGMakie.jl is a visualisation-only package for causal diagrams on Makie and GraphMakie. It draws what the user or an upstream identification package already specified; it does not compute identification certificates, fit models, or run simulations. See also BOUNDARIES.md and the shared DESIGN_PRINCIPLES.md.","title":"Scope & design boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Ownership-across-the-CDCS-stack","page":"Scope & boundaries","text":"Package Role Boundary with DAGMakie\nDAGMakie.jl (this package) Causal DAG / digraph / mixed-graph figures, layered & temporal layouts, themes, edge routing, display conventions Core depends only on Graphs, Makie, GraphMakie, NetworkLayout, and Reexport\nCausalDynamics.jl Structural graphs, identify certificates, TemporalUnrolling, SCM/CDM simulation Supplies IdentificationResult and TemporalUnrolling via its CausalDynamicsDAGMakieExt weakdep\nCausalInference.jl d-separation and adjustment-set search Optional weakdep (DAGMakieCausalInferenceExt) for highlighting when loaded\nCausalTargeted.jl / CausalMediation.jl LMTP, Super Learner, Bayesian g-computation, and mediation EIF estimation Out of scope here; stress notebooks call dagplot for figures only","title":"Ownership across the CDCS stack"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#What-is-in-scope","page":"Scope & boundaries","text":"Layered & SCC-aware layouts: deterministic layered layout for acyclic graphs, SCC-aware layout with curved feedback overlays for cyclic graphs, and NetworkLayout fallbacks.\nTemporal & skeleton layouts: dagplot_time_indexed, dagplot_temporal, and dagplot_skeleton. Temporal glyphs read declared value_representation (:interval_summary only uses rounded rectangles); support and referent identity are never inferred from shape.\nCausal conventions & visual grammar: observed vs latent styling, bidirected confounding (MixedGraph), dagitty-style color_by role colouring, display-only do(·) surgery (do_surgery, dagplot_do), interaction IDAGs, and DiD SWIGs.","title":"What is in scope"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#What-is-out-of-scope","page":"Scope & boundaries","text":"Computing adjustment sets, d-separation, or identify in core (belongs in CausalInference.jl / CausalDynamics.jl).\nStatistical estimation, Super Learner, LMTP, or mediation EIF.\nCohort data, registries, or hierarchical model fitting.\nInventing ontology from glyphs or layout (:occasion / :enduring belong in book prose and CausalDynamics.ReferentSpec; node count follows temporal_support + graph_kind).\nHard dependency on CausalDynamics, CausalInference, or Graphviz (dot).\nInteractive WebGL graph exploration for massive graphs (target scale is notebook- and publication-scale causal diagrams, roughly 5–200 labelled nodes).","title":"What is out of scope"},{"category":"section","location":"scope/#Validation-checks","page":"Scope & boundaries","text":"Geometry & API regressions: test/test_layout.jl, test/test_temporal_layout.jl, test/test_auto_align.jl, test/test_highlighting.jl, and test/test_interventions.jl.\nOptional extension tests: test/test_smart.jl and test/test_causaldynamics_ext.jl.\nPackage QA: Aqua.jl ambiguity and piracy checks in test/test_aqua.jl.\nOptional timing budgets: set DAGMAKIE_PERF=1 when running the test suite (test/test_perf.jl), or run the manual timing gallery via julia --project=. test/benchmark_layouts.jl.","title":"Validation checks"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Bidirected-Edges","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"Bidirected edges (↔) represent unmeasured common causes (latent confounders) in causal diagrams.","title":"Bidirected Edges"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#The-MixedGraph-Type","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"MixedGraph supports both directed (→) and bidirected (↔) edges:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Create empty mixed graph\nmg = MixedGraph(3)\n\n# Add directed edges\nadd_directed_edge!(mg, 1, 2)  # X → Y\nadd_directed_edge!(mg, 2, 3)  # Y → Z\n\n# Add bidirected edge (unmeasured confounding)\nadd_bidirected_edge!(mg, 1, 3)  # X ↔ Z\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg, labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"The MixedGraph Type"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Creating-Mixed-Graphs","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"","title":"Creating Mixed Graphs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#From-Scratch","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"using Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nmg = MixedGraph(2)\nadd_directed_edge!(mg, 1, 2)\nadd_bidirected_edge!(mg, 1, 2)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"From Scratch"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#From-Edge-Lists","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"using Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nmg = mixed_graph(3,\n    [(1, 2), (2, 3)],    # Directed edges\n    [(1, 3)]              # Bidirected edges\n)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"From Edge Lists"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#From-Existing-DiGraph","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"using Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\nmg = MixedGraph(g, [(1, 3)])  # Add bidirected X ↔ Z\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(mg; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"From Existing DiGraph"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Customising-Bidirected-Edges","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot(mg,\n    labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"],\n    bidirected_color = :red,\n    bidirected_width = 1.5,\n    bidirected_style = :dash,\n    bidirected_curvature = 0.4,\n    bidirected_arrow_size = 10,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Customising Bidirected Edges"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Common-Confounded-Patterns","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"DAGMakie provides convenience functions for common confounded structures. Each uses a pedagogical default layout so bidirected arcs are not obscured; pass layout=... to override.","title":"Common Confounded Patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Simple-Confounding","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot_confounded([\"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Simple Confounding"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Frontdoor-Criterion","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot_frontdoor([\"X\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Frontdoor Criterion"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Instrumental-Variable-with-Confounding","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot_iv_confounded([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Instrumental Variable with Confounding"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#M-Bias","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"The classic five-node form with explicit latents (the letter M). Conditioning on the collider M opens X  U₁  M  U₂  Y. The ADMG projection of the same structure is X  M  Y.\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_m_bias()  # U₁, U₂, X, M, Y\nfig\nFigure()","title":"M-Bias"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Graph-Constructors","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"Get the underlying mixed graph objects:\n\nmg, labels = confounded_graph([\"X\", \"Y\"])\nmg, labels = frontdoor_graph([\"X\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\nmg, labels = iv_confounded_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nmg, labels = m_bias_graph()                 # U₁, U₂, X, M, Y (explicit latents)\nspec = m_bias_spec()                        # same DAG with Latent / Collider styling","title":"Graph Constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/bidirected/#Querying-Bidirected-Edges","page":"Bidirected edges","text":"# Check if bidirected edge exists\nhas_bidirected_edge(mg, 1, 2)  # true/false\n\n# Get all bidirected edges\nbi_edges = bidirected_edges(mg)  # Set of (i, j) tuples\n\n# Count bidirected edges\nn = num_bidirected_edges(mg)","title":"Querying Bidirected Edges"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Basic-Plotting","page":"Basic plotting","text":"Core plotting entry points and customisation options.\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Classic confounding triangle: Z → X → Y, Z → Y\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])","title":"Basic Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#The-dagplot-Function","page":"Basic plotting","text":"The main entry point is dagplot, which creates a new figure:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g; kwargs...)\n\nFor plotting into an existing axis, use dagplot!:\n\np = dagplot!(ax, g; kwargs...)","title":"The dagplot Function"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Node-Customisation","page":"Basic plotting","text":"","title":"Node Customisation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Size-and-Colour","page":"Basic plotting","text":"By default, label_position = :outer draws compact steel-blue nodes (OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE) with dark external labels (OUTER_LABEL_COLOR). For in-node white labels, pass label_position = :inner (with node_size = 34 or fit_node_size_to_labels = true), and adjust nlabels_color when you choose light node fills:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = labels,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    node_size = 34,\n    node_color = [:goldenrod, :seagreen, :steelblue],\n    nlabels_color = :white,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Size and Colour"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Outline-Styling","page":"Basic plotting","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = labels,\n    node_strokewidth = 2.0,\n    node_strokecolor = :black,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Outline Styling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Edge-Customisation","page":"Basic plotting","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = labels,\n    edge_color = :gray,\n    edge_width = 1.5,\n    arrow_size = 12,\n    arrow_shift = :end,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Edge Customisation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Label-Customisation","page":"Basic plotting","text":"","title":"Label Customisation"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Basic-Labels","page":"Basic plotting","text":"By default, label_position = :outer places dark labels outside compact nodes in the largest angular gap. For in-node labels, pass label_position = :inner (which centres white text in 34-pixel fills); for manual external placement without auto-alignment, pass label_position = :inner with a positive nlabels_distance and a non-centred nlabels_align: GraphMakie offsets along the align direction, so (:center, :center) leaves the pixel offset at zero and the text stays inside the marker. Prefer black text off the fill:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 280))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"In-node (:inner)\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Manual external align\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g;\n    labels = labels,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    nlabels_fontsize = 16,\n    nlabels_color = :white,\n    nlabels_distance = 0,\n)\ndagplot!(ax2, g;\n    labels = labels,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    nlabels_color = :black,\n    nlabels_distance = 12,\n    # (:center, :bottom) anchors the bottom of the text, so labels sit above\n    nlabels_align = (:center, :bottom),\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Basic Labels"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Label-Alignment","page":"Basic plotting","text":"nlabels_align is the Makie text-box anchor (passed through GraphMakie), not “put the label on this side of the node”. The named side is which edge of the label sits on the anchor point, so the node ends up on that side of the label:\n\nnlabels_align Anchor meaning Label ends up…\n(:left, :center) left edge of text on point right of the node\n(:right, :center) right edge of text on point left of the node\n(:center, :bottom) bottom of text on point above the node\n(:center, :top) top of text on point below the node\n\nWith a positive nlabels_distance, GraphMakie also shifts the anchor along the same direction (so (:left, :center) both left-anchors and nudges east).\n\nBy default labels sit outside the node in the largest angular gap (label_position = :outer): compact markers (OUTER_LABEL_NODE_SIZE), dark text, and auto-aligned anchors. Pass label_position = :inner with fit_node_size_to_labels = true for in-node white labels whose marker size follows each label (short names stay round; wider ones become ovals):\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng2, labels2 = confounding_graph([\"nutrition\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g2;\n    labels = labels2,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    fit_node_size_to_labels = true,\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nManual anchors (typically with a positive nlabels_distance when labels sit outside the fill). Panel titles show where the label appears relative to the node:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 280))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"(:right, :bottom) → label NW of node\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Per-node anchors\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g;\n    labels = labels,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    nlabels_distance = 12,\n    nlabels_color = :black,\n    nlabels_align = (:right, :bottom),\n)\ndagplot!(ax2, g;\n    labels = labels,\n    label_position = :inner,\n    nlabels_distance = 12,\n    nlabels_color = :black,\n    # Z right-anchored → label west; X top-anchored → label south; Y left-anchored → label east\n    nlabels_align = [(:right, :center), (:center, :top), (:left, :center)],\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Label Alignment"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Layout","page":"Basic plotting","text":"DAGMakie now defaults to an automatic layout strategy:\n\nlayout_mode = :auto chooses a deterministic layered layout for acyclic graphs\nlayout_mode = :cyclic uses an SCC-aware layout with curved feedback edges\nlayout_mode = :spring falls back to a force-directed layout\nlayout = ... still accepts explicit node positions or a NetworkLayout.jl layout object\nWithin-layer node_gap defaults to 2.6 for label_position=:inner and 1.8 for :outer (override with node_gap=…)\nEdges are straight by default; curve forks or skip chords with :curved, CurvedEdge(), or a bow fraction (long_edge_routing = :quadratic when curved)\nPer-edge overrides: edge_routing = Dict((1, 4) => :curved, (1, 5) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.28)) (:straight, :curved, a bow fraction, or CurvedEdge(distance=…) for bend angle)\nComparison / intervention helpers reuse a shared DAGLayoutResult so panels do not jump","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Robustness-and-routing-defaults","page":"Basic plotting","text":"DAGMakie targets notebook- and publication-scale causal diagrams (roughly 5–200 labelled nodes) that read closer to Graphviz dot / dagitty than to a generic spring layout:\n\nCurved edge styles: long_edge_routing defaults to :quadratic Bézier samples for bowed edges; alternatives include :natural_cubic, :none, :rounded, :tangents, and :curve_distance.\nFeedback & bidirected arcs: feedback edges inside an SCC use curved overlays with boundary-aware arrowheads; bidirected confounding arcs are clipped to node markers.\nLabel-aware axis limits: computed bounds include markers, labels, and overlay waypoints so external labels and curves are not clipped.\nHighlight precedence: HighlightSpec uses first listing wins when the same node or edge appears in multiple layers (list exposure / outcome / adjustment styling before path colours).\n\nFor CPDAG skeletons and occasion×variable grids, see Skeletons & Time (dagplot_skeleton, dagplot_time_indexed).\n\nYou can still use NetworkLayout.jl directly when you want a force-directed layout:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g, labels = labels, layout_mode = :spring)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nusing NetworkLayout\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 280))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Spring()\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Stress()\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g; labels = labels, layout = Spring())\ndagplot!(ax2, g; labels = labels, layout = Stress())\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Robustness & routing defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Figure-Size","page":"Basic plotting","text":"fig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = labels,\n    figure_size = (480, 320),    # Width × Height in pixels\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Figure Size"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Structural-edge-labels-(LaTeX)","page":"Basic plotting","text":"GraphMakie elabels pass through dagplot / dagplot!. Use structural_edge_labels to annotate edges with linear-SCM structural weights from a matrix B (with B_ij the parameter on j to i), or with short TeX fragments of a mechanism, optionally as Makie LaTeXStrings. A non-zero diagonal is treated as a self-loop: by default structural_edge_labels adds any missing i → i edges on g before labelling (layout still treats the loopless core as a DAG). Prefer a dedicated copy of the graph if later chunks must keep the original edge set:\n\nB = [\n    0.0  0.0  0.0;\n    0.8  0.0  0.0;\n    0.5  1.2  3.0;\n]\ng_loop, labels_loop = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g_loop;\n    labels = labels_loop,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_loop, B; digits = 1),  # adds Y → Y\n    elabels_fontsize = 14,\n    elabels_distance = 12,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,   # keep maths upright\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nCustom TeX on edges (still one label per Graphs.edges(g) entry):\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n    labels = labels,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(\n        g,\n        [\"\\\\beta_{ZX}\", \"\\\\beta_{ZY}\", \"\\\\beta_{XY}\"];\n        latex = true,\n    ),\n    elabels_fontsize = 14,\n    elabels_distance = 12,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    padding = 0.45,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Structural edge labels (LaTeX)"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Signed-edge-terminals","page":"Basic plotting","text":"Use edge_weights when a diagram should distinguish an explicitly declared inhibitory mechanism from an ordinary directed mechanism. Negative values draw an inhibitory -| terminal; non-negative values retain the usual arrowhead. The values are display metadata: DAGMakie does not infer a mechanism sign from the graph or from an edge label.\n\nPass either one value per Graphs.edges(g) entry or the structural matrix B used by structural_edge_labels:\n\nB_signed = [\n    0.0   0.0  0.0;\n    0.8   0.0  0.0;\n    0.5  -1.2  0.0;\n]\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g;\n    labels = labels,\n    edge_weights = B_signed,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g, B_signed; digits = 1),\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    padding = 0.45,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Signed edge terminals"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/basic/#Padding","page":"Basic plotting","text":"Control spacing around the graph:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = labels,\n    padding = 0.15,              # 15% padding (fraction of range)\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Padding"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Prerequisites","page":"Getting Started","text":"DAGMakie requires a Makie backend. CairoMakie is typical for static figures:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CairoMakie\")\nPkg.add(\"Graphs\")","title":"Prerequisites"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#A-chain-DAG","page":"Getting Started","text":"using Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\n# Create a simple chain: A → B → C\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\n# Plot with labels\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g, labels = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"])\nfig\nFigure()\n\ndagplot returns:\n\nfig: the Figure\nax: the Axis\np: the GraphPlot (node positions and plot attributes)\n\nBy default, labels sit outside compact nodes (label_position = :outer). Pass label_position = :inner with fit_node_size_to_labels = true for in-node white text (see Basic Plotting).","title":"A chain DAG"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Edge-labels-(structural-parameters)","page":"Getting Started","text":"GraphMakie elabels pass through dagplot. Prefer structural_edge_labels so labels follow Graphs.edges(g) order and optional LaTeX maths (path coefficients or short mechanism fragments):\n\ng_w, labels_w = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\n# B[i,j] = structural weight on j → i\nB = [\n    0.0  0.0  0.0;\n    0.8  0.0  0.0;\n    0.5  1.2  0.0;\n]\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g_w;\n    labels = labels_w,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_w, B; digits = 1),\n    elabels_fontsize = 14,\n    elabels_distance = 12,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    padding = 0.45,\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSymbolic TeX on the same topology:\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g_w;\n    labels = labels_w,\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(\n        g_w,\n        [\"\\\\beta_{ZX}\", \"\\\\beta_{ZY}\", \"\\\\beta_{XY}\"];\n        latex = true,\n    ),\n    elabels_fontsize = 14,\n    elabels_distance = 12,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    padding = 0.45,\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSee Basic Plotting for more elabels_* options.","title":"Edge labels (structural parameters)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Saving-figures","page":"Getting Started","text":"save(\"my_dag.png\", fig)           # PNG\nsave(\"my_dag.pdf\", fig)           # PDF (vector)\nsave(\"my_dag.svg\", fig)           # SVG (vector)","title":"Saving figures"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Common-patterns","page":"Getting Started","text":"Convenience constructors cover the five canonical three-variable causal motifs:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (1100, 220))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Chain\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Fork\")\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], title = \"Collider\")\nax4 = Axis(fig[1, 4], title = \"Confounding\")\nax5 = Axis(fig[1, 5], title = \"Mediation\")\n\ng_chain, _ = chain_graph([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ng_fork, _ = fork_graph([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ng_collider, _ = collider_graph([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\n# Triangle layout is applied automatically for confounding and mediation\ng_conf, _ = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\ng_med, _ = mediation_graph([\"X\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\n\ndagplot!(ax1, g_chain; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ndagplot!(ax2, g_fork; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ndagplot!(ax3, g_collider; labels = [\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\ndagplot!(ax4, g_conf; labels = [\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\ndagplot!(ax5, g_med; labels = [\"X\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nEach pattern also has a stand-alone one-liner returning (fig, ax, p):\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_chain([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_fork([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_collider([\"X\", \"Y\", \"Z\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_confounding([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_mediation([\"X\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\n\nConfounding, mediation, and mixed-graph helpers (dagplot_frontdoor, dagplot_iv_confounded, etc.) use fixed triangle layouts by default so shortcut and bidirected edges remain visible. Pass layout=... to override.\n\nTo compose multiple panels in a single Figure, pass Axis objects to dagplot!:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (720, 260))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"Panel A\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"Panel B\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g1; labels = labels1)\ndagplot!(ax2, g2; labels = labels2)","title":"Common patterns"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Curved-edges-(skip-chords)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Edges are straight unless you pass edge_routing. Curve a fork or skip chord with :curved, CurvedEdge, or a bow fraction (fraction of chord length):\n\ng_m = DiGraph(4)\nadd_edge!(g_m, 1, 2); add_edge!(g_m, 1, 3); add_edge!(g_m, 1, 4)\nadd_edge!(g_m, 2, 3); add_edge!(g_m, 2, 4); add_edge!(g_m, 3, 4)\nlabels_m = [\"W\", \"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"]\npositions = [Point2f(0, 0), Point2f(1.2, 0), Point2f(1.2, 0.9), Point2f(2.4, 0)]\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g_m;\n    labels = labels_m,\n    layout = positions,\n    edge_routing = Dict(\n        (1, 4) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.18, side = :right),\n        (1, 3) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.12),\n    ),\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Curved edges (skip chords)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Temporal-glyphs","page":"Getting Started","text":"Rounded rectangles mean value_representation = :interval_summary only. Single-node supports (from-onset attributes) stay circles unless you pass that representation:\n\nkeys = [(:pasture, nothing), (:burden, 0)]\ng = SimpleDiGraph(2)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot_temporal(\n    g,\n    keys;\n    onset_times = [0, 0],\n    value_representations = [:attribute, :interval_summary],\n    labels = [\"pasture\", \"burden\"],\n    figure_size = (420, 220),\n    fit_node_size_to_labels = false,\n)\n@assert p[:node_marker][][1] == :circle\n@assert p[:node_marker][][2] == interval_summary_node_marker()\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSee Skeletons & Time and Terminology.","title":"Temporal glyphs"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Signed-and-cyclic-biological-feedback","page":"Getting Started","text":"Pass edge_weights to distinguish activating (→, positive) from inhibitory (-|, negative) influences, and layout_mode = :cyclic when a biological diagram contains a reciprocal feedback loop (such as host immunity suppressing parasite load):\n\n# 1=Infection (I), 2=Immunity (M), 3=Condition (C)\n# I → M (+0.7), M -| I (-0.6), I -| C (-0.5)\nB_fb = [\n     0.0  -0.6   0.0;\n     0.7   0.0   0.0;\n    -0.5   0.0   0.0;\n]\ng_fb = graph_from_structural_matrix(B_fb)\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(\n    g_fb;\n    labels = [\"I\", \"M\", \"C\"],\n    edge_weights = B_fb,\n    layout_mode = :cyclic,\n    title = \"Host–parasite feedback (inhibitory -|)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Signed and cyclic biological feedback"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Difference-in-differences-(BACI)-SWIG","page":"Getting Started","text":"In a before–after-control-impact (BACI) or difference-in-differences study, the parallel-trends assumption is a statement about the untreated potential outcome Yₜ(0) rather than the factual DAG alone. dagplot_did_swig() pairs the factual 2×2 DAG with its Single-World Intervention Graph (Richardson & Robins, 2013; see Visual Grammar):\n\nfig = dagplot_did_swig()\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Difference-in-differences (BACI) SWIG"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#See-also","page":"Getting Started","text":"Basic Plotting — node and edge appearance\nNode Types & Styling — semantic node types\nVisual Grammar — IDAGs, SWIGs, modifier edges\nSkeletons & Time — CPDAG skeletons and time grids\nCausal Analysis — d-separation, adjustment sets, and dagitty-style color_by colouring","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Literature underpinning DAGMakie.jl's causal-diagram conventions, layout rules, and visual grammar. BibTeX keys match the CDCS book references.bib where present.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Causal-diagrams,-good-controls,-and-do(·)-surgery","page":"References","text":"Pearl, J. (2009). Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press, Cambridge. — key pearl2009causality (structural diagrams, d-separation, and do(·) graph surgery)\nCinelli, C., Forney, A., & Pearl, J. (2024). A crash course in good and bad controls. Sociological Methods & Research, 53(3), 1071–1104. doi:10.1177/00491241221099552 — key cinelli2024goodbadcontrols (canonical good-control vs bad-control layouts)\nHernán, M. A., & Robins, J. M. (2020). Causal Inference: What If. Chapman & Hall/CRC, Boca Raton, FL. — key hernan2020causal (longitudinal and time-varying confounding diagrams)","title":"Causal diagrams, good controls, and do(·) surgery"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Role-colouring-and-adjustment-sets","page":"References","text":"Textor, J., van der Zander, B., Gilthorpe, M. S., Liśkiewicz, M., & Ellison, G. T. H. (2016). Robust causal inference using directed acyclic graphs: the R package 'dagitty'. International Journal of Epidemiology, 45(6), 1887–1894. doi:10.1093/ije/dyw341 (ancestor and adjustment role colouring used by color_by)\nvan der Zander, B., Liśkiewicz, M., & Textor, J. (2019). Separators and adjustment sets in causal graphs: complete criteria and an algorithmic framework. Artificial Intelligence, 270, 1–40. doi:10.1016/j.artint.2018.12.006 — key vanderzander2019separators (arXiv:1803.00116) (upstream algorithm in CausalInference.jl / CausalDynamics.jl)","title":"Role colouring and adjustment sets"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#SWIGs-and-interaction-diagrams","page":"References","text":"Richardson, T. S., & Robins, J. M. (2013). Single world intervention graphs (SWIGs): A unification of the counterfactual and graphical approaches to causality. arXiv:1201.5658 — key richardson2013single (split-node intervention grammar for dagplot_did_swig and SwigFixed)","title":"SWIGs and interaction diagrams"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Related-software","page":"References","text":"Julia plotting & graphs: Makie.jl, GraphMakie.jl, Graphs.jl\nJulia causal stack: CausalDynamics.jl, CausalTargeted.jl, CausalMediation.jl, CausalInference.jl\nR companions: dagitty, ggdag\nNarrative companion: CDCS Book","title":"Related software"},{"category":"section","location":"terminology/#Terminology","page":"Terminology","text":"DAGMakie keeps Pearl-style plot and surgery names (Intervention, do_surgery, find_backdoor_paths, …). Display surgery does not change the simulation model.\n\nTemporal declarations follow CausalDynamics: glyphs follow value_representation (rounded only for :interval_summary), not ontology and not single-node support. Whitehead glossary terms belong in the CDCS book Concept Reference, not in this manual.\n\nTerm Meaning in this package\nPointwise node (v, t) One node per discrete time; default circle glyph\nSingle-node support (v, nothing) Reused node (FromOnsetSupport, …); placed at onset; circle by default (shape ≠ support)\nInterval summary glyph Rounded rectangle only when value_representation = :interval_summary (interval_summary_node_marker)\nOnset Horizontal placement for single-node keys (onset_times)\ngraph_kind Annotate time-unrolled vs process vs semantic figures\nDeclared relation_kind CausalDynamics edge semantics (:causal_influence, :constitutive_persistence, :constitutive_dependence, :participation); the sole input to causal_projection\nEdge role (temporal_edge_role) From CausalDynamics temporal_edge_records: non-causal relation_kind if declared, or construction label (:onset_assignment, :recurrent_influence, :pointwise_influence, :causal_influence); styling/provenance only\nreferent_id lanes Optional identity connectors; not graph edges and not cut by do_surgery\n\nPlacement follows the key shape alone: (v, t) sits at column t, (v, nothing) at its onset. There is no separate node-mode flag. Glyphs and layout must not change identification meaning, and must not be read as ontology.","title":"Terminology"},{"category":"section","location":"terminology/#Display-vs-identify","page":"Terminology","text":"DAGMakie reuses Pearl plot names (Intervention, CausalQuery, do_surgery) for display surgery only. Prefer the DAGMakie. qualifier in mixed sessions. Identification and generative do(·) remain in CausalDynamics. See the Policy taxonomy in the shared DESIGN_PRINCIPLES.","title":"Display vs identify"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Node-Types-and-Styling","page":"Node types & styling","text":"DAGMakie provides semantic node types for causal diagrams, with automatic styling.","title":"Node Types & Styling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Node-Types","page":"Node types & styling","text":"The NodeType enum defines common roles in causal diagrams:\n\n@enum NodeType begin\n    Observed      # Standard observed variable\n    Latent        # Unobserved/latent variable\n    Treatment     # Treatment/exposure (thick stroke)\n    Outcome       # Outcome (emphasised outline)\n    Instrument    # Instrumental variable\n    Confounder    # Confounding variable\n    Mediator      # Mediating variable\n    Collider      # Collider variable\n    EffectMeasure # IDAG causal-effect node\n    SwigFixed     # Fixed half of a SWIG split\nend\n\nFor interaction / DiD conventions (IDAGs, modifier edges, SWIGs — defined there), see Visual Grammar.","title":"Node Types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Using-DAGSpec","page":"Node types & styling","text":"The DAGSpec type combines a graph with node specifications:\n\nusing Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # Z → X\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # Z → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # X → Y\n\nspec = DAGSpec(g,\n    node_labels = [\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"],\n    node_types = [Confounder, Treatment, Outcome]\n)\n\n# Auto triangle layout: confounder Z at the apex\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(spec)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Using DAGSpec"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Node-Type-Styling","page":"Node types & styling","text":"Each node type has default styling (in-node white labels on dark fills, except Latent / SwigFixed):\n\nType Default Colour Marker / stroke Use Case\nObserved Steel blue circle Standard observed variables\nLatent Gray (hollow) circle, stroke 2 Unobserved variables\nTreatment Steel blue circle, stroke 2.5 Exposure / intervention\nOutcome Steel blue circle, stroke 2.0 darkgray Response variables\nInstrument Goldenrod diamond Instrumental variables\nConfounder Goldenrod circle Confounding variables\nMediator Seagreen circle Mediating variables\nCollider Medium purple circle Collider variables\nEffectMeasure Seagreen rect IDAG effect-measure node\nSwigFixed White rect, stroke 2 SWIG fixed half a","title":"Node Type Styling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Custom-Styling-Functions","page":"Node types & styling","text":"Override default styling:\n\n# Get default colour for a type\ncolor = default_node_color(Treatment)  # :steelblue\n\n# Get default marker\nmarker = default_node_marker(EffectMeasure)  # :rect\n\n# Get default stroke width\nstroke = default_node_strokewidth(Treatment)  # 2.5","title":"Custom Styling Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Apply-Node-Type-Styling","page":"Node types & styling","text":"Apply styling to existing graphs:\n\ng = SimpleDiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)\n\ntypes = [Confounder, Treatment, Outcome]\ncolors, markers, strokewidths = apply_node_type_styling(types)\n\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(g,\n    labels = [\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"],\n    node_color = colors,\n    node_marker = markers,\n    node_strokewidth = strokewidths,\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Apply Node Type Styling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Pre-typed-Graph-Constructors","page":"Node types & styling","text":"Create graphs with pre-assigned node types:\n\nspec = typed_confounding_graph()\nfig, ax, p = dagplot(spec)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 240))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"typed_mediation\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"typed_instrumental\")\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], title = \"typed_collider\")\ndagplot!(ax1, typed_mediation_graph())\ndagplot!(ax2, typed_instrumental_graph())\ndagplot!(ax3, typed_collider_graph())\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Pre-typed Graph Constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/styling/#Style-Presets","page":"Node types & styling","text":"DAGMakie provides style presets for different contexts:\n\ng, labels = confounding_graph([\"Z\", \"X\", \"Y\"])\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 240))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], title = \"default_style\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"minimal_style\")\nax3 = Axis(fig[1, 3], title = \"bold_style\")\nax4 = Axis(fig[1, 4], title = \"presentation_style\")\ndagplot!(ax1, g; labels = labels, style = default_style())\ndagplot!(ax2, g; labels = labels, style = minimal_style())\ndagplot!(ax3, g; labels = labels, style = bold_style())\ndagplot!(ax4, g; labels = labels, style = presentation_style())\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Style Presets"}]
}
